window.NARR={"1":[{"t":["Good morning, everyone, and welcome to our first lecture in medical terminology.","Medicine has its own language, and today you will learn how that language works.","The good news is that medical words are not random.","Many medical terms are built using meaningful parts, including a root and a suffix, with an optional prefix.","Once you understand these building blocks, long and frightening words become easy to read.","Let us begin."],"s":[0,4.25,9.22,12.76,19.15,24.11],"f":[0,4.1,8.88,12.29,18.44,23.22]},{"t":["Here is what you should be able to do by the end of today.","First, identify the word root or roots, the suffix, and any optional prefix.","Second, work out the meaning of a new word using a simple three-step method.","Third, recognise the common roots for the main body systems.","And fourth, use the common suffixes and prefixes, and then test yourself with twenty multiple-choice questions at the end."],"s":[0,4.95,9.54,14.49,18.03],"f":[0,4.71,9.08,13.79,17.16]},{"t":["Part one is about building medical words.","Many medical terms can be understood by separating meaningful word parts, although this approach does not work for every term.","We will study prefixes, roots, and suffixes, learn a practical way to analyse unfamiliar medical terms, and build our first words together."],"s":[0,2.71,10.45],"f":[0,2.6,10.03]},{"t":["Look at this slide carefully, because it is the foundation of the whole course.","A prefix, when present, appears at the beginning and changes the meaning of the root.","For example, tachy means fast, and hypo means under or low.","The word root carries the core meaning. It may name a body part, a substance, or a function. For example, cardi means heart and neur means nerve.","A suffix comes at the end and can indicate a condition, a specialty, or a procedure. For example, itis means inflammation and ectomy means surgical removal.","A combining form consists of a root and a combining vowel, usually o. Thus, cardi is the root, o is the combining vowel, and cardio is the combining form.","For cardiology, we combine cardio with logy. Before a suffix that begins with a vowel, the combining vowel is often omitted. For example, cardi plus itis gives carditis."],"s":[0,5.37,11.13,15.35,25.72,35.7,46.83],"f":[0,5.09,10.54,14.54,24.36,33.81,44.35]},{"t":["Now, how do you read a word you have never seen?","Use this three-step trick: end, beginning, middle.","Take pericarditis.","Step one, read the suffix first: itis means inflammation, so it tells you what is happening.","Step two, read the prefix: peri means around, so it tells you where.","Step three, read the root: card means heart, so it tells you which body part.","Put it together: pericarditis means inflammation of the pericardium, the sac surrounding the heart."],"s":[0,4.37,7.16,7.95,14.31,19.48,25.44],"f":[0,4.07,6.66,7.4,13.31,18.12,23.67]},{"t":["Let us build some words together.","Hyper, meaning excessive, plus glyc, meaning sugar, plus emia, indicating a condition in the blood, gives hyperglycemia, an abnormally high blood glucose concentration.","Hypo plus thyroid plus ism gives hypothyroidism, an underactive thyroid gland.","Cardio plus logy gives cardiology, the study of the heart.","Anti plus bio plus tic gives antibiotic. In clinical use, antibiotics are medicines used to treat bacterial infections.","And neuro plus pathy gives neuropathy, disease or dysfunction of the nerves.","Notice the tip: a dash shows where the part attaches."],"s":[0,2.7,13.05,18,22.5,30.6,36],"f":[0,2.59,12.5,17.24,21.55,29.3,34.48]},{"t":["Part two looks at roots, organised by body system.","The root names the body part, so the best way to learn roots is system by system: the heart, vessels and blood, the respiratory system, the digestive, urinary and reproductive systems, and then the nervous, musculoskeletal and endocrine systems."],"s":[0,3.57],"f":[0,3.2]},{"t":["We start with the heart, vessels and blood.","Cardio means heart, as in cardiology and cardiomegaly.","Angio and vaso mean vessel, as in angiography and vasoconstriction.","Hemato and hemo mean blood, as in hematology and hematuria.","Arterio means artery, as in arteriosclerosis.","Phlebo and veno mean vein, as in phlebitis and venous.","And thrombo means clot, as in thrombosis.","Notice how one root can build many different words."],"s":[0,2.67,6.89,11.76,16.22,19.97,23.78,26.83],"f":[0,2.53,6.3,10.8,14.81,18.19,21.62,24.38]},{"t":["Now the respiratory system, the airway and lungs.","Pulmono means lung. Pneumo can mean lung or air, depending on the term, as in pneumonia or pneumothorax.","Broncho means bronchus, as in bronchitis and bronchoscopy.","Tracheo is the trachea, or windpipe, as in tracheostomy.","Laryngo is the larynx, or voice box, as in laryngitis.","Pharyngo is the pharynx, or throat, as in pharyngitis.","Rhino and naso mean nose, as in rhinitis and nasal.","Pleuro refers to the pleura, the membranes lining the chest wall and covering the lungs, as in pleuritis.","Remember that the meaning of pneumo depends on the context."],"s":[0,3.39,11.03,14.42,18.24,22.48,26.3,30.54,38.18],"f":[0,3.17,10.31,13.48,17.05,21.02,24.59,28.55,35.69]},{"t":["Here is a useful way to remember these roots: follow the path of the air.","Air enters through the nose, rhino or naso, then passes through the pharynx, the larynx, the trachea, the bronchi, and the bronchioles before reaching the alveoli, where gas exchange occurs.","For example, laryngo plus itis gives laryngitis, inflammation of the larynx, the voice box."],"s":[0,5.8,17.41],"f":[0,5.61,16.84]},{"t":["Next, the digestive and hepatobiliary system, including the liver, gallbladder and bile ducts.","Gastro means stomach, as in gastritis and gastroscopy.","Entero means intestine, as in enteritis.","Colo and colono mean colon, as in colitis and colonoscopy.","Hepato means liver, as in hepatitis and hepatomegaly.","Cholecysto means gallbladder, as in cholecystitis.","Cholangio refers to the bile ducts, as in cholangiography, an imaging examination of the bile ducts.","Pancreato means pancreas, as in pancreatitis.","And procto refers to the rectum and anus, as in proctology."],"s":[0,6.32,10.21,13.13,17.99,21.88,24.8,32.57,35.49],"f":[0,5.99,9.68,12.44,17.05,20.73,23.5,30.87,33.63]},{"t":["Again, follow the path, this time of food.","Food passes from the mouth through the pharynx and esophagus into the stomach, and then through the small intestine and large intestine, including the colon.","Look at the three examples.","Gastro plus itis is gastritis, inflammation of the stomach.","Hepato plus megaly is hepatomegaly, an enlarged liver.","And colono plus scopy is colonoscopy, looking inside the colon."],"s":[0,3.04,12.54,14.44,17.86,20.9],"f":[0,3.11,12.83,14.77,18.27,21.38]},{"t":["The urinary system includes the organs that make and remove urine.","Nephro and reno mean kidney, as in nephrology and renal.","Pyelo is the renal pelvis, as in pyelonephritis.","Uretero is the ureter, as in ureterolithiasis.","Cysto and vesico mean the bladder, as in cystitis and vesical.","And urethro is the urethra, as in urethritis.","Look at the word builder: pyelo plus nephro plus itis gives pyelonephritis, inflammation of the kidney and renal pelvis.","And uretero plus lith plus iasis gives ureterolithiasis, a stone in the ureter."],"s":[0,4.59,8.52,12.33,16.31,20.61,24.17,32.24],"f":[0,4.16,7.77,11.25,14.88,18.8,22.17,29.61]},{"t":["Now the reproductive system.","For the female, hystero and metro mean the uterus, as in hysterectomy and endometrium, and oophoro means the ovary, as in oophorectomy.","For the male, prostato means the prostate, as in prostatitis, and orcho or orchido means the testis, as in orchitis.","Read the examples: hyster plus ectomy is surgical removal of the uterus, and orch plus itis is inflammation of the testis."],"s":[0,2.12,11.69,19.9],"f":[0,1.96,10.65,18.07]},{"t":["The nervous system covers the brain, the spinal cord, the nerves and the mind.","Neuro means nerve, as in neurology and neuropathy.","Encephalo means brain, as in encephalitis.","Myelo means the spinal cord or the bone marrow, as in myelopathy.","And psycho means the mind, as in psychology.","Be careful with myelo, because it has two meanings.","The context of the word tells you which one is intended."],"s":[0,4.9,8.68,12.02,16.72,20.04,23.35],"f":[0,4.23,7.46,10.39,14.7,17.72,20.66]},{"t":["The musculoskeletal system includes bones, joints, muscles and cartilage.","Osteo means bone, as in osteology.","Arthro means joint, as in arthritis.","Myo means muscle, as in myalgia.","And chondro means cartilage, as in chondritis.","Read the example on the slide: myo, muscle, plus algia, pain, gives myalgia, which simply means muscle pain."],"s":[0,5.35,8.43,11.37,14.03,17.24],"f":[0,4.66,7.23,9.78,12.06,14.97]},{"t":["The endocrine system is made of glands that release hormones into the blood.","Endocrino refers to the endocrine glands, as in endocrinology.","Thyroido is the thyroid gland, as in hypothyroidism.","Adreno is the adrenal gland, as in adrenalectomy.","And glyco means sugar, or glucose, as in hyperglycemia.","Read it: hyper means excessive, glyc means sugar, and emia refers to a blood condition. Hyperglycemia means an abnormally high concentration of glucose in the blood."],"s":[0,5.81,9.83,13.41,16.98,21],"f":[0,5.27,8.91,12.15,15.39,19.04]},{"t":["Part three is about common suffixes.","Suffixes sit at the end of the word, and they tell you what is happening.","We will group them to make them easier to learn: inflammation, disease and symptoms; then tumors, growth and hardening; and finally breakdown, softening and procedures."],"s":[0,2.8,6.81],"f":[0,2.53,6.08]},{"t":["Here is the first group of suffixes.","For inflammation and disease: itis means inflammation, as in arthritis; osis means an abnormal condition, as in cyanosis; pathy means disease, as in neuropathy; and emia means a blood condition, as in anemia.","For symptoms and signs: algia and dynia refer to pain, as in neuralgia. The suffix rrhea refers to flow or discharge, as in diarrhea. The separate suffixes rrhage and rrhagia refer to excessive bleeding or hemorrhage. Phobia means fear, as in claustrophobia."],"s":[0,3.15,17.97],"f":[0,2.91,16.65]},{"t":["The second group covers growth, numbers and hardening.","Oma means a tumor or mass, as in lipoma.","Megaly means enlargement, as in cardiomegaly.","Plasia means formation or development, as in hyperplasia, an increase in the number of cells.","Trophy means growth or nourishment, as in hypertrophy.","Penia means deficiency, as in leukopenia, while cytosis commonly indicates an increased number of a particular cell type, as in leukocytosis.","Stenosis means narrowing, as in aortic stenosis, and sclerosis means hardening, as in arteriosclerosis."],"s":[0,3.82,8.11,10.97,18.13,21.95,31.97],"f":[0,3.56,7.57,10.24,16.92,20.49,29.84]},{"t":["The third group covers breakdown and softening.","Lysis means breakdown or destruction, as in hemolysis.","Malacia means softening, as in osteomalacia.","Necrosis means irreversible pathological death of cells or tissue.","And cele means a hernia, a bulging out, as in meningocele.","On the right are the procedure suffixes: ectomy, surgical removal, as in hysterectomy; scopy, looking inside, as in gastroscopy; ostomy, making an opening, as in tracheostomy; graphy, recording an image, as in angiography; and logy, the study of a subject, as in cardiology."],"s":[0,3.32,7.12,9.97,14.24,19.46],"f":[0,3.18,6.82,9.55,13.65,18.65]},{"t":["Part four is about common prefixes.","Prefixes sit at the beginning of a word and change the meaning of the root.","The next five slides teach them in ten groups: direction; inside, outside and change; speed and function; self, same and different; how much of the body; position; time and order; number; size and amount; and finally prefixes that mean not or against."],"s":[0,2.81,7.38],"f":[0,2.51,6.61]},{"t":["The first group is about direction.","Ab means away from, as in abduction, and ad means toward, as in adduction.","Circum means around, as in circumoral, around the mouth.","Para means beside or near, as in the parathyroid glands, beside the thyroid.","And retro means behind, as in retrosternal, behind the sternum.","The second group is about inside, outside and change.","Endo means within, as in endocardium.","Exo means outside, as in exocrine.","Meso means middle, as in mesoderm. Ecto means outer, as in ectoderm. Meta can mean change, after, or beyond, depending on the term. Metastasis refers to the spread of a cancer to a distant site."],"s":[0,2.68,8.93,12.96,18.76,23.23,27.25,29.93,32.61],"f":[0,2.52,8.39,12.17,17.62,21.82,25.59,28.11,30.63]},{"t":["Now speed and function.","Brady means slow, as in bradycardia, and tachy means fast, as in tachypnea.","Dys can mean abnormal, difficult, or painful, as in dysuria. Eu means good or normal, as in eupnea, meaning normal breathing.","Iso means equal or the same, as in isotonic.","Then self, same and different: auto means self, as in autoimmune; homo means same, as in homogeneous; hetero means different, as in heterogeneous; allo means other, as in allograft; and homeo means same or steady, as in homeostasis."],"s":[0,1.8,7.66,17.12,21.18],"f":[0,1.79,7.61,17.02,21.05]},{"t":["These prefixes tell you how much of the body is involved, and where.","Uni means one; hemi means half or one side, as in hemiplegia; multi means many; semi means partly, as in semiconscious; and pan means all, as in pansinusitis.","Uni, as in unilateral, means one side only.","For position: sub means under or below, as in subcutaneous; epi means upon, as in epidermis; intra means within, as in intravenous; inter means between, as in intercostal; and trans means across or through, as in transdermal."],"s":[0,5.68,17.93,21.42],"f":[0,5.69,17.95,21.45]},{"t":["Next, prefixes of time, sequence, and number.","Pre means before, as in prenatal, and post means after, as in postoperative.","Neo means new, as in neonatal, and re means again or back. Remember that peri means around and is a position prefix, not a time prefix.","For number: mono means one, bi means two, as in bilateral, and tri means three, as in tricuspid.","Poly means many or much, as in polyuria, too much urine, and oligo means few or scanty, as in oliguria, too little urine."],"s":[0,3.1,8.86,20.37,28.34],"f":[0,3.04,8.68,19.97,27.79]},{"t":["The last group covers size and amount, and saying no.","Micro means small, as in microcyte, a small red blood cell. Macro means large, as in macrocephaly, an abnormally large head.","Hyper means too much or high, as in hyperglycemia, and hypo means too little or low, as in hypoglycemia.","Pan means all, as in pancytopenia.","The prefixes a and an mean without or absence of, as in apnea, cessation of breathing.","Anti means against, as in antibiotic, de means removal or loss, as in dehydration, dis means apart, as in dislocation, and contra means against or opposite, as in contraindication, a reason not to give a treatment."],"s":[0,4.32,13.4,21.61,24.2,31.12],"f":[0,4.18,12.97,20.92,23.43,30.13]},{"t":["Let us summarise today in four ideas.","A prefix, when present, comes first and modifies the meaning, such as hypo for low or tachy for fast.","The root carries the central meaning, which may refer to a body part, a substance, or a function, such as cardi or glyc.","A suffix appears at the end and may indicate a condition or procedure, such as itis or ectomy.","To analyse a medical term, begin with the suffix, then any prefix, and finally the root or roots. Remember that not every term contains all three parts."],"s":[0,2.51,9.31,17.54,23.99],"f":[0,2.47,9.17,17.28,23.62]},{"t":["Now it is time to check yourself.","There are twenty multiple-choice questions, five on each slide, and the answer key is at the end.","My tip for you: read the suffix first.","It will often lead you straight to the right answer."],"s":[0,2.4,8.2,10.82],"f":[0,2.17,7.4,9.94]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 1 to 5.","Question 1. Which word means inflammation of the liver? A, Hepatitis. B, Hepatoma. C, Hepatology. D, Hepatomegaly.","Question 2. The root cardi o means: A, blood. B, heart. C, lung. D, skin.","Question 3. Which suffix means surgical removal? A, -itis. B, -algia. C, -ectomy. D, -logy.","Question 4. Which word means study of the heart? A, Carditis. B, Cardiopathy. C, Cardiomegaly. D, Cardiology.","Question 5. The prefix sub- means: A, under. B, around. C, above. D, before."],"s":[0,2.6,16.32,26.43,38.56,51.66],"f":[0,2.53,14.96,24.48,35.8,47.78]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. Which word means low blood sugar? A, Hyperglycemia. B, Hypoglycemia. C, Glycosuria. D, Hematology.","Question 7. The suffix -algia means: A, pain. B, incision. C, disease. D, swelling.","Question 8. Which word means enlarged liver? A, Hepatitis. B, Hepatoma. C, Hepatology. D, Hepatomegaly.","Question 9. Which root means kidney? A, neur/o. B, nephr/o. C, gastr/o. D, oste/o.","Question 10. Which word means difficult breathing? A, apnea. B, bradycardia. C, dyspnea. D, edema."],"s":[0,2.6,16.32,27.22,40.37,51.65],"f":[0,2.45,15.05,25.19,37.31,47.87]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 11 to 15.","Question 11. The suffix -osis means: A, surgical repair. B, looking inside. C, blood condition. D, abnormal condition.","Question 12. Which word means inflammation of the bladder? A, Cystitis. B, Cystectomy. C, Cystology. D, Cytology.","Question 13. The prefix tachy- means: A, slow. B, fast. C, under. D, around.","Question 14. Which word means white blood cell? A, erythrocyte. B, thrombocyte. C, leukocyte. D, myocyte.","Question 15. The suffix -logy means: A, disease. B, pain. C, incision. D, study of."],"s":[0,3.14,15.97,29.66,40.26,52.89],"f":[0,2.68,14.57,27.04,37.1,48.9]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 16 to 20.","Question 16. Which word means disease of the nerves? A, neuralgia. B, neuropathy. C, neuritis. D, neurology.","Question 17. The prefix peri- means: A, within. B, after. C, around. D, below.","Question 18. Which word means a tool to look inside the stomach? A, gastroscope. B, gastrotomy. C, gastritis. D, gastric.","Question 19. The suffix -scopy means: A, surgical puncture. B, recording. C, looking inside. D, nourishment.","Question 20. Which word means within the vein? A, intramuscular. B, subcutaneous. C, intercostal. D, intravenous."],"s":[0,2.99,16.11,26.87,40.54,52.67],"f":[0,2.71,14.79,24.88,37.63,49.02]},{"t":["Answer key.","Question 1: A, Hepatitis.","Question 2: B, heart.","Question 3: C, -ectomy.","Question 4: D, Cardiology.","Question 5: A, under.","Question 6: B, Hypoglycemia.","Question 7: A, pain.","Question 8: D, Hepatomegaly.","Question 9: B, nephr/o.","Question 10: C, dyspnea.","Question 11: D, abnormal condition.","Question 12: A, Cystitis.","Question 13: B, fast.","Question 14: C, leukocyte.","Question 15: D, study of.","Question 16: B, neuropathy.","Question 17: C, around.","Question 18: A, gastroscope.","Question 19: C, looking inside.","Question 20: D, intravenous."],"s":[0,1.31,4.28,6.78,9.57,12.61,15.22,18.6,21.28,24.37,27.12,29.95,33.43,36.48,39.33,42.46,45.51,48.7,51.74,54.92,58.31],"f":[0,1.21,4.02,6.46,9.04,11.86,14.32,17.41,19.9,22.76,25.38,28.03,31.17,33.99,36.78,39.64,42.4,45.3,48.12,51.03,54.18]}],"2":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture two, body organization.","Today we move from the language of words to the map of the body.","We will see how the body is built, step by step, from tiny cells to tissues, to organs, and to the whole person.","We will also learn the standard words doctors use to describe position, direction and location, so that everyone describes the body in exactly the same way."],"s":[0,3.18,7.32,14.31],"f":[0,2.75,6.38,12.8]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","Describe the six structural levels from chemicals to an organism, and recognize population as an additional ecological level.","Name the main cell types and the four basic tissues.","Use anatomical position, body regions, planes, cavities and directional terms correctly.","And know the main routes of drug administration.","As always, you will finish by testing yourself with twenty multiple-choice questions."],"s":[0,5.42,12.38,16.25,20.51,23.6],"f":[0,5.26,12.01,15.77,19.9,22.9]},{"t":["Part one is about the levels of organization.","The body is built in steps, and each level is made from the one before it.","Cells form tissues, tissues form organs, organs form systems, and systems form the whole organism.","The first six levels describe organization within an individual organism. The seventh, population, is an ecological level beyond the individual."],"s":[0,3.31,9.94,16.16],"f":[0,3.05,9.16,14.89]},null,{"t":["Part two is about cells and tissues.","We will compare prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, look at specialised cells and their jobs, and then learn the four basic tissue types that build every organ in the body."],"s":[0,2.61],"f":[0,2.39]},{"t":["All living things are made of cells, but not all cells are the same.","Prokaryotic cells have no true nucleus; their DNA lies in a region called the nucleoid.","They have no membrane-bound organelles, and they are small and simple.","Bacteria are the classic example.","Eukaryotic cells have a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.","Many animal cells lack a rigid cell wall and vary in shape. Plant cells have cellulose cell walls; photosynthetic plant cells also contain chloroplasts. Plant cells are not always rectangular.","Our own cells are eukaryotic."],"s":[0,5.66,11.73,16.17,18.2,21.83,33.97],"f":[0,5.29,10.95,15.1,16.99,20.39,31.72]},{"t":["Each specialised cell has a shape that matches its job.","The neuron carries electrical signals.","The muscle cell contracts to make movement.","The epithelial cell covers and protects surfaces.","The red blood cell carries oxygen using hemoglobin.","The white blood cell defends against infection.","The sperm cell fertilises the egg, and the egg cell supports early development.","Remember the examples at the bottom: a neuron is long so it can carry signals far, and a red blood cell is a flexible disc so it can squeeze through tiny vessels."],"s":[0,3.77,6.57,9.33,12.87,16.83,19.85,24.74],"f":[0,3.38,6.03,8.49,11.84,15.22,17.98,22.5]},null,{"t":["Here are some examples of these tissues.","Epithelial cells come in three shapes: squamous, which are flat; cuboidal, which are cube-shaped; and columnar, which are tall.","Connective tissue has very different forms, including bone, cartilage, fat and even blood.","And remember the chain at the bottom: cells build tissues, tissues build organs, organs build organ systems, and organ systems build the human body."],"s":[0,2.69,10.18,16.09],"f":[0,2.22,9.07,14.53]},{"t":["Part three is about position and body regions.","A standard position and named regions allow everyone to describe the body in the same way.","We will cover the anatomical position, the axial and appendicular regions, and the regions of the head, neck and trunk."],"s":[0,3.39,9.19],"f":[0,3.12,8.41]},{"t":["The anatomical position is the standard reference position for describing the body.","The person stands upright, facing forward, with the arms at the sides, palms facing forward, and feet directed forward and parallel.","Why does this matter?","Because every directional term is based on this position.","Whatever position the patient is actually lying in, we describe the body as if it were in anatomical position, so we all speak the same language."],"s":[0,4.41,12.12,13.59,16.9],"f":[0,4.12,11.32,12.7,15.79]},{"t":["The body is divided into two main parts.","The axial region is the central axis: the head, the neck and the trunk.","The appendicular region is the limbs: the upper limb and the lower limb.","The limbs attach to the axial body through girdles.","The pectoral girdle is at the shoulder, and the pelvic girdle is at the hip."],"s":[0,2.99,7.54,11.8,15.28],"f":[0,2.78,6.89,10.82,13.99]},{"t":["The head is called the cephalic region, and the neck is called the cervical region.","You will meet these adjectives often.","For example, cephalic refers to the head, and cervical refers to the neck.","Be careful: cervical can also refer to the cervix of the uterus, so the context tells you which one is meant."],"s":[0,4.83,7.26,11.94],"f":[0,4.33,6.57,10.95]},{"t":["The trunk includes the thoracic region, which is the chest, the abdominal region, and the pelvic region.","The abdominopelvic surface is commonly divided into nine regions.","In the top row: the right hypochondriac, the epigastric, and the left hypochondriac.","In the middle row: the right lumbar, the umbilical, and the left lumbar.","In the bottom row: the right iliac, the hypogastric, and the left iliac.","Remember that these regions are surface areas.","They help us describe where a structure or a pain is located."],"s":[0,6.55,10.02,15.03,20.04,25.05,27.75],"f":[0,6.28,9.6,14.4,19.19,23.99,26.58]},{"t":["Part four covers planes, cavities and directions.","In other words, how we cut, divide and point to places in the body.","We will learn the body planes, the body cavities, the directional terms, and the abdominal quadrants and regions."],"s":[0,3.35,7.62],"f":[0,3.08,7.08]},{"t":["Body planes are flat, imaginary slices that divide the body.","The sagittal plane divides the body into right and left parts.","The frontal plane, also called the coronal plane, divides it into a front, or anterior, part and a back, or posterior, part.","And the transverse plane, also called the horizontal plane, divides it into upper, superior, and lower, inferior, parts.","These planes are used all the time in imaging, for example in CT and MRI scans."],"s":[0,4.4,8.73,16.97,24.8],"f":[0,4.21,7.94,15.36,22.66]},{"t":["Body cavities are spaces inside the body that hold and protect the organs.","There are two major cavities.","The dorsal cavity is at the back.","It contains the cranial cavity, which holds the brain, and the vertebral or spinal cavity, which holds the spinal cord.","The ventral cavity is at the front.","It contains the thoracic cavity, with the heart and lungs, and the abdominopelvic cavity, with the digestive and pelvic organs."],"s":[0,5.05,7.23,9.56,17.24,19.59],"f":[0,4.88,6.78,8.91,15.75,17.97]},{"t":["The ventral cavity is divided into smaller spaces.","The thoracic cavity contains the right and left pleural cavities, each enclosing a lung, while the mediastinum lies between them. Within the mediastinum, the pericardial cavity is the narrow space between the layers of the serous pericardium around the heart.","The diaphragm forms the floor of the chest and separates it from the abdomen.","Below it, the abdominopelvic cavity has an abdominal part, containing the liver, stomach and intestines, and a pelvic part, containing the bladder and reproductive organs."],"s":[0,3.01,18.07,23.34],"f":[0,2.86,17.18,22.2]},{"t":["Directional terms tell us where one part is compared with another.","Superior, or cranial, means toward the head.","Inferior, or caudal, means toward the feet.","Anterior, or ventral, means toward the front.","Posterior, or dorsal, means toward the back.","Medial means toward the midline, lateral means away from the midline, and intermediate means between medial and lateral."],"s":[0,4.14,7.26,10.23,13.31,16.32],"f":[0,3.73,6.55,9.33,12.03,14.94]},{"t":["Here are more directional terms.","For the limbs: proximal means nearer to the trunk, and distal means farther from the trunk.","For depth: superficial means near the surface, and deep means away from the surface.","For sides: ipsilateral means on the same side, like the left shoulder and the left wrist, and contralateral means on opposite sides, like the right shoulder and the left knee."],"s":[0,2.2,7.56,12.88],"f":[0,1.92,6.81,11.5]},{"t":["Let us see these terms in action.","The skull is superior to the neck vertebrae.","The sternum is anterior to the heart.","The nose is medial to the eyes, and the eyes are lateral to the nose.","The middle finger is intermediate, between the index finger and the ring finger.","The elbow is proximal to the wrist, and the wrist is distal to the elbow.","And the skin is superficial to the muscles, while the muscles are deep to the skin."],"s":[0,2.15,5.08,7.71,12.26,16.79,21.41],"f":[0,1.93,4.57,7.04,11.12,15.41,19.64]},{"t":["More pairs in action.","The left lung and the left kidney are on the same side of the body, so they are ipsilateral; the left lung and the right kidney are on opposite sides, so they are contralateral.","The ears are bilateral because they are on both sides. The vermiform appendix is a single structure, usually situated in the right lower abdomen, although its position can vary.","The heart is internal to the ribcage, and the ribcage is external to the heart.","The spinal cord is central, and the nerves are peripheral.","On the hand, the palm is the palmar surface and the back of the hand is dorsal.","On the foot, the sole is plantar and the top of the foot is dorsal."],"s":[0,1.24,11.79,20.79,25.44,28.55,33.82],"f":[0,1.22,11.55,20.37,24.93,27.97,33.14]},{"t":["A simple way to divide the abdomen is into four quadrants.","Two lines pass through the navel, one vertical and one horizontal.","This gives the right upper quadrant, the left upper quadrant, the right lower quadrant, and the left lower quadrant.","Doctors use the quadrants every day to describe the location of pain and to organise the physical examination."],"s":[0,3.89,8.12,14.45],"f":[0,3.55,7.33,13.01]},{"t":["For more precise location, we use the nine abdominopelvic regions, a three by three grid.","The upper row is the right hypochondriac, the epigastric, and the left hypochondriac regions.","The middle row is the right lumbar, the umbilical, and the left lumbar regions.","The lower row is the right iliac, the hypogastric, and the left iliac regions."],"s":[0,6.2,12.7,17.74],"f":[0,5.44,11.29,15.74]},{"t":["Part five is about the routes of drug administration, in other words, how a medicine gets into the body to reach the place where it acts.","Common groups include enteral routes involving the gastrointestinal tract, injectable parenteral routes, and other routes such as inhalation and transdermal delivery. Sublingual drugs are absorbed through the oral mucosa rather than being swallowed."],"s":[0,9.65],"f":[0,9.44]},{"t":["Enteral means through the gut.","The oral route, written P O, means the drug is swallowed.","Sublingual, S L, means under the tongue, where the drug can be absorbed across the oral mucosa without first passing through the stomach.","Rectal, P R, means into the rectum.","Parenteral routes bypass the gut.","Intradermal, I D, means injection into the dermis, the skin layer beneath the epidermis.","Subcutaneous, S C, is into the fat beneath the skin.","Intramuscular, I M, is into the muscle.","And intravenous, I V, is directly into a vein.","Notice how the prefixes you learned in lecture one, like sub and intra, tell you exactly where the drug goes."],"s":[0,1.94,6.21,15.13,17.84,19.78,25.21,29.09,31.81,35.3],"f":[0,1.86,5.97,14.54,17.15,19.01,24.23,27.96,30.57,33.93]},{"t":["Here are more parenteral routes.","Intra-articular means into a joint space.","Intrathecal medication is delivered into cerebrospinal fluid, usually in the subarachnoid space within the spinal canal.","Epidural means into the epidural space, outside the dura mater and inside the vertebral canal.","Inhalation delivers a drug through the respiratory tract; it is not an injection route.","Transdermal means it is absorbed through the skin, usually from a patch.","And topical means it is applied directly to the skin or to mucous membranes."],"s":[0,1.96,4.32,10.6,16.49,21.99,26.7],"f":[0,1.85,4.06,9.97,15.51,20.68,25.12]},{"t":["Now it is time to check yourself.","There are twenty multiple-choice questions, five on each slide, and the answer key is at the end of the lecture."],"s":[0,2.37],"f":[0,2.17]},{"t":["Questions one to five are about the levels of organization and the tissues.","Pause the lecture now and choose your answers.","Think about which level comes first, what the basic unit of life is, and which tissue covers surfaces and which one supports and binds.","Then continue."],"s":[0,4.84,7.73,16.13],"f":[0,4.45,7.11,14.44]},{"t":["Questions six to ten.","Pause and answer them.","Think about which cell has no true nucleus, which plane divides right from left, front from back, and upper from lower, and what we call the standard reference position.","Then continue."],"s":[0,1.87,3.57,12.98],"f":[0,1.76,3.25,11.65]},{"t":["Questions eleven to fifteen are about the body cavities.","Pause the lecture and answer them.","Remember: the brain sits in the cranial cavity, the spinal cord in the vertebral cavity, and both of these belong to the dorsal cavity.","Then continue."],"s":[0,3.77,6.04,14.26],"f":[0,3.41,5.48,13.13]},{"t":["The final questions, sixteen to twenty, test the directional terms.","Pause and choose your answers.","Remember: anterior is front, posterior is back, proximal is nearer the trunk, and distal is farther away.","Then continue to the answer key."],"s":[0,4.48,6.59,13.43],"f":[0,4.05,5.91,12.23]},{"t":["Let us check your answers.","One is A, the chemical level.","Two is B, the cell.","Three is C, tissue.","Four is D, epithelial tissue.","Five is A, connective tissue.","Six is C, the prokaryotic cell.","Seven is A, sagittal.","Eight is B, frontal or coronal.","Nine is C, transverse.","Ten is D, anatomical position.","Eleven is B, the cranial cavity.","Twelve is A, the vertebral cavity.","Thirteen is B, the dorsal body cavity.","Fourteen is B, the thoracic cavity.","Fifteen is B, the abdominopelvic cavity.","Sixteen is C, anterior.","Seventeen is B, posterior.","Eighteen is C, proximal.","Nineteen is A, distal.","And twenty is D, inferior.","Well done, and see you in the next lecture."],"s":[0,1.84,4.1,5.73,7.38,9.91,12.19,14.86,16.77,19.41,21.43,24.08,26.75,29.26,32.38,35.31,38.51,40.64,42.92,44.91,46.94,49.12],"f":[0,1.66,3.77,5.25,6.72,9.18,11.4,13.89,15.72,18.13,20.03,22.48,24.9,27.29,30.06,32.63,35.53,37.48,39.69,41.54,43.31,45.34]}],"3":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture three, the skeletal and muscular systems.","Together, bones, joints and muscles allow us to stand, to move, and to protect our internal organs.","In this lecture you will learn the words for these structures, the main bones and muscles, the types of joints and movements, and the common disorders, tests, procedures and drugs."],"s":[0,3.82,10.55],"f":[0,3.48,9.76]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","Use skeletal and muscle word roots, and describe bone and muscle tissue.","Name the main bones and muscles of the axial and appendicular body.","Classify joints, and describe body movements such as flexion, abduction and rotation.","And recognise the main disorders, tests, procedures and drugs.","Then you will test yourself with forty questions."],"s":[0,3.71,8.29,12.78,18.36,22.64],"f":[0,3.36,7.55,11.68,16.9,20.75]},{"t":["Let us start with the skeletal word roots.","Osteo means bone, as in osteology, osteoporosis and osteomyelitis.","Arthro means joint, as in arthritis, arthroscopy and polyarthralgia, which is pain in many joints.","Chondro means cartilage, as in chondritis and chondroma.","And myo means muscle, as in myalgia, myopathy and myositis.","These four roots will appear again and again in this lecture."],"s":[0,2.69,8.38,15.31,19.3,23.94],"f":[0,2.47,7.36,13.72,17.25,21.48]},{"t":["Here are three more roots.","The combining form periost-o refers to the periosteum, the membrane covering the outer surface of bone, apart from articular surfaces, as in periostitis.","Osteo also appears in words about bone growth, such as osteogenesis, the formation of bone, and osteoblast, the cell that builds bone.","And calcaneo refers to the heel bone, the calcaneus, as in calcaneal."],"s":[0,2.17,12.16,21.71],"f":[0,1.93,10.79,19.27]},{"t":["Now read these words with me.","Osteo plus porosis gives osteoporosis, porous and weak bone.","Arthro plus itis is arthritis, inflammation of a joint.","Chondro plus oma is chondroma, a benign tumor of cartilage.","Myo plus algia is myalgia, muscle pain.","Osteo plus genesis is osteogenesis, the formation of bone.","And arthro plus scopy is arthroscopy, looking inside a joint with a camera."],"s":[0,2.19,6.81,11.1,15.7,19.04,23.89],"f":[0,1.91,6.33,10.22,14.31,17.25,21.53]},{"t":["Bones are grouped by their shape.","Long bones are longer than they are wide, like the femur.","Short bones are cube-shaped, like the carpal bones of the wrist.","Flat bones are thin and protect organs, like the sternum.","Irregular bones have complex shapes, like the vertebrae.","And sesamoid bones grow inside tendons; the best example is the patella, the kneecap."],"s":[0,2.15,5.65,9.64,13.59,17.24],"f":[0,1.95,5.18,8.77,12.22,15.49]},{"t":["This slide shows the structure of a typical long bone, such as the femur.","The epiphysis is each rounded end, and the diaphysis is the long shaft in the middle.","Articular cartilage covers the joint surface.","The periosteum is the outer membrane of the bone.","The medullary cavity, inside the shaft, holds yellow marrow.","And the endosteum is the thin inner lining of that cavity."],"s":[0,4.59,10.19,13.48,16.79,20.79],"f":[0,4.13,9.18,12.07,15.05,18.67]},{"t":["Four types of cell build, maintain and remodel bone.","The osteoprogenitor cell is the stem cell.","The osteoblast builds new bone.","The osteocyte maintains mature bone.","And the osteoclast breaks bone down.","A simple memory aid: blast builds, clast cuts.","The bone matrix has two parts: an organic part, called osteoid, made mainly of type one collagen, which gives flexibility, and an inorganic mineral part, hydroxyapatite, which gives hardness."],"s":[0,3.62,6.68,9.11,11.91,14.56,17.94],"f":[0,3.25,5.92,8.11,10.68,13.07,16.08]},{"t":["The skeleton has two divisions.","The axial skeleton has eighty bones: the twenty-two bones of the skull, including the mandible, the six auditory ossicles, the hyoid bone, the adult vertebral column with twenty-six bones, the sternum and twelve pairs of ribs.","It provides support and protection.","The appendicular skeleton has one hundred and twenty-six bones: the shoulder girdle, which is the clavicle and the scapula, the upper limbs, the pelvic girdle, which is the hip bones, and the lower limbs.","It is mainly for movement and walking."],"s":[0,1.92,15.71,17.63,30.66],"f":[0,1.83,14.97,16.8,29.21]},{"t":["The skull has eight cranial bones that protect the brain: the frontal bone, two parietal bones, two temporal bones, the occipital, the sphenoid and the ethmoid.","It also has fourteen facial bones that build the face, including the nasal bones, the maxillae, the zygomatic bones, the lacrimal and palatine bones, the inferior nasal conchae, the vomer, and the mandible, the lower jaw."],"s":[0,10.1],"f":[0,9.1]},{"t":["The vertebral column has twenty-six bones in five regions, from the neck to the tailbone.","There are seven cervical vertebrae in the neck, twelve thoracic vertebrae in the chest, and five lumbar vertebrae in the lower back.","Below them is the sacrum, made of fused vertebrae, and finally the coccyx, the tailbone.","A useful memory aid for the numbers is breakfast at seven, lunch at twelve, and dinner at five."],"s":[0,5.55,13.53,19.2],"f":[0,5.08,12.31,17.68]},{"t":["The thoracic cage protects the heart and lungs.","It is made of the sternum and twelve pairs of ribs.","The sternum has three parts: the manubrium at the top, the body in the middle, and the xiphoid process at the tip.","Ribs one to seven are true ribs, each connected to the sternum by its own costal cartilage.","Ribs eight to ten are false ribs that connect indirectly to the sternum through the cartilage above. Ribs eleven and twelve are floating ribs without an anterior attachment to the sternum."],"s":[0,2.6,6.18,13.33,18.86],"f":[0,2.47,5.88,12.68,17.94]},{"t":["The shoulder girdle joins the arm to the axial skeleton.","It is formed by the clavicle, or collarbone, and the scapula, or shoulder blade.","The upper limb includes the humerus in the arm, the radius and ulna in the forearm, and the bones of the hand: eight carpals, five metacarpals and fourteen phalanges, giving twenty-seven bones in each hand."],"s":[0,3.75,8.93],"f":[0,3.4,7.98]},{"t":["The pelvic girdle joins the legs to the axial skeleton.","Each hip bone is formed by three parts: the ilium, the ischium and the pubis, which meet at the acetabulum, the hip socket.","The lower limb includes the femur, the patella, the tibia and the fibula, and the bones of the foot: seven tarsals, five metatarsals and fourteen phalanges, giving twenty-six bones in each foot."],"s":[0,3.8,11.57],"f":[0,3.41,10.43]},{"t":["Joints can be classified structurally by the tissue linking the bones and whether a joint cavity is present. Their functional mobility varies with joint type.","Fibrous joints have no cavity and are joined by fibrous tissue; they are immovable or slightly movable.","The sutures of the skull and the gomphosis that holds a tooth are immovable, while the syndesmosis between the radius and ulna allows slight movement.","Cartilaginous joints also have no cavity; they are joined by cartilage and are immovable or slightly movable.","A synchondrosis, such as the joint between the first rib and the sternum, is immovable, while a symphysis, such as an intervertebral disc or the pubic symphysis, is slightly movable.","Synovial joints have a synovial cavity and an articular capsule, and they are freely movable, like the knee."],"s":[0,9.16,15.38,24.54,30.77,41.76],"f":[0,8.67,14.57,23.24,29.14,39.55]},{"t":["Synovial joints come in six types, and each allows a different kind of movement.","Plane joints allow sliding, as between some wrist bones.","Hinge joints move in one direction, like the elbow.","Pivot joints allow rotation, like the joint between the first two neck vertebrae that lets you turn your head.","Condyloid joints move in two directions, like the wrist.","Saddle joints also move in two directions, like the base of the thumb.","And ball and socket joints move in all directions, like the shoulder and the hip."],"s":[0,4.91,8.68,11.9,18.57,22,26.47],"f":[0,4.48,7.73,10.89,16.96,20.24,24.24]},{"t":["Now the bone disorders.","Osteoporosis is low bone density with fragile bones.","Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of existing bone, typically in adults. Rickets is defective mineralization at the growing bone and growth plates in children and can cause bowed legs. They are related but not identical conditions.","Osteomyelitis is a bone infection.","Osteosarcoma is a malignant bone tumor.","A fracture is a break in a bone.","And Paget disease is abnormal bone remodelling.","Look at the word parts at the bottom: porosis means porous, malacia means softening, and sarcoma means a malignant tumor."],"s":[0,1.68,5.03,19.69,21.79,24.3,27.66,30.59],"f":[0,1.55,4.64,18.17,20.1,22.42,25.51,28.22]},{"t":["Joint disorders cause pain, swelling and stiffness.","Arthritis is inflammation of a joint.","Osteoarthritis is loss of the joint cartilage, often with age.","Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune arthritis.","Gout is arthritis caused by urate crystals.","Bursitis is an inflamed bursa, the small fluid sac near a joint.","And dislocation means the joint is completely out of place, while subluxation means it is partly out of place.","Look at the clinical words on the right: arthro means joint, chondro means cartilage, burso means bursa, synovio means the synovial membrane, luxation means dislocation, and itis means inflammation."],"s":[0,3.37,6.47,10.94,14.84,18.26,22.93,30.01],"f":[0,3.02,5.78,9.83,13.11,16.14,20.36,26.72]},{"t":["The spine can develop abnormal curves.","Scoliosis is an abnormal lateral curvature of the spine, commonly accompanied by vertebral rotation.","Excessive thoracic kyphosis is an abnormally increased posterior convexity; normal thoracic kyphosis is physiological.","Excessive lumbar lordosis, or hyperlordosis, is a greater than normal inward curve; some lumbar lordosis is physiological.","A herniated disc can compress or irritate a nerve, while spinal stenosis means narrowing of the spinal canal.","And remember the difference: a sprain is an injury to a ligament, while a strain is an injury to a muscle or tendon."],"s":[0,2.64,8.8,14.95,22.43,30.35],"f":[0,2.49,8.28,14.08,21.12,28.58]},{"t":["Fractures are described by how the bone breaks.","In a simple, or closed, fracture, the skin is intact.","In an open, or compound, fracture, the skin is broken.","A greenstick fracture is an incomplete break, common in children.","A comminuted fracture means the bone is broken into many pieces.","The spine words at the bottom are useful too: spondylo and vertebro mean vertebra, kypho means humpback, lordo means curve, scolio means crooked, and stenosis means narrowing."],"s":[0,3.17,6.71,10.38,14.61,18.95],"f":[0,2.88,6.12,9.54,13.38,17.23]},{"t":["We use several imaging studies for bones and joints.","An X-ray shows fractures.","A CT scan gives cross-section images.","MRI shows the bone marrow and the soft tissues very well.","A bone scan shows areas of high bone activity.","And DEXA measures bone density, so it is the test we use to diagnose osteoporosis."],"s":[0,3.53,5.63,8.84,12.79,16.26],"f":[0,3.17,5.11,8.04,11.5,14.58]},{"t":["Some diagnostic procedures take samples or look inside joints.","Bone marrow aspiration samples the liquid marrow.","A bone biopsy samples solid bone tissue.","And arthroscopy places a camera inside a joint, so the doctor can see the joint directly."],"s":[0,4.45,7.92,11.1],"f":[0,4.13,7.24,10.18]},{"t":["Fracture management has two goals: put the bone back in place, and hold it still while it heals.","Reduction realigns the bone.","A cast holds the bone still.","Traction applies steady pulling.","Internal fixation uses plates and screws placed inside the body.","And external fixation uses a frame outside the body, connected to the bone by pins."],"s":[0,5.82,8.07,10.21,12.34,16.97],"f":[0,5.02,7.08,9,11.02,15.19]},{"t":["Surgery can repair joints and bones.","Arthroscopy is keyhole joint surgery.","Joint replacement puts in an artificial joint, for example a hip or knee replacement.","Bone grafting uses bone to repair bone.","And spinal fusion joins two or more vertebrae together, so they no longer move against each other."],"s":[0,2.72,5.61,11.16,14.05],"f":[0,2.54,5.16,10.36,13.04]},{"t":["For pain and inflammation we use analgesics, NSAIDs and corticosteroids.","For bone health we use calcium supplements, and vitamin D, which helps calcium absorption.","Remember the caution on the slide: these medicines should be used only under medical supervision."],"s":[0,4.17,10],"f":[0,4.2,10.09]},{"t":["Now we move to the muscles, starting with the word roots.","Myo and musculo mean muscle, as in myalgia, muscle pain, myopathy, muscle disease, and musculoskeletal, relating to the muscles and the skeleton.","Fascio refers to the fascia, the sheet of tissue around the muscles, as in fasciitis, inflammation of the fascia."],"s":[0,3.53,13.15],"f":[0,3.14,12.01]},{"t":["Three more muscle roots.","Teno means tendon, as in tenotomy, cutting a tendon, and tenodesis, fixing a tendon surgically.","The combining form sarc-o means flesh. Sarcoma is a malignant tumor of mesenchymal tissues, and the sarcolemma is the cell membrane of a muscle fiber. The root itself does not specifically mean connective tissue.","And kinesio means movement, as in kinesiology, the study of movement, and dyskinesia, abnormal movement."],"s":[0,1.75,8.31,23.18],"f":[0,1.68,7.99,22.28]},null,{"t":["This slide shows the major superficial muscles of the body.","On the front, the anterior view, you can see the frontalis on the forehead, the sternocleidomastoid in the neck, the deltoid on the shoulder, the pectoralis major on the chest, the biceps brachii and brachioradialis in the arm, the rectus abdominis and external oblique on the abdomen, and the sartorius, quadriceps femoris and tibialis anterior in the leg.","On the back, the posterior view, you can see the trapezius, the deltoid, the triceps brachii, the latissimus dorsi, the erector spinae, the gluteus maximus, the hamstrings, the gastrocnemius and the soleus."],"s":[0,4.01,25.95],"f":[0,3.73,23.27]},{"t":["Here we focus on the head, neck and trunk.","On the face are the frontalis, the temporalis, the orbicularis oculi around the eye, the zygomaticus, and the masseter, a powerful chewing muscle.","In the neck is the sternocleidomastoid.","On the trunk are the pectoralis major, the intercostal muscles between the ribs, the rectus abdominis and the external oblique.","On the back are the trapezius, the latissimus dorsi, the erector spinae, which keeps the spine upright, and the gluteus maximus."],"s":[0,2.8,12.23,14.95,23.27],"f":[0,2.42,10.8,13.28,20.5]},{"t":["These limb muscles are important for movement and posture.","In the upper limb: the deltoid lifts the arm, the biceps brachii bends the elbow, and the triceps brachii straightens it.","The brachioradialis also helps bend the elbow, and the forearm flexors and extensors move the wrist and fingers.","In the lower limb: the gluteus medius and maximus at the hip, the sartorius, the adductor group, and the quadriceps femoris at the front of the thigh; the hamstrings at the back; and in the leg, the tibialis anterior, the fibularis longus, the gastrocnemius and the soleus."],"s":[0,3.58,11.28,18.62],"f":[0,3.45,10.36,17.19]},{"t":["Now the major joint movements.","Flexion decreases the angle at a joint, it is bending, like bending the elbow.","Extension increases the angle, it is straightening.","Abduction moves a part away from the midline, like lifting the arm out to the side.","Adduction moves it back toward the midline.","And rotation turns a part around its long axis, as at the trunk or the hip."],"s":[0,2.23,7.17,10.36,15.52,18.21],"f":[0,2.05,6.8,9.74,14.3,16.84]},{"t":["Five more movements.","Circumduction is a circular movement, like drawing a circle with the arm at the shoulder.","Pronation turns the forearm so the palm faces down, and supination turns it so the palm faces up.","At the ankle, dorsiflexion brings the foot up toward the shin, and plantar flexion points the foot down, as when you stand on your toes."],"s":[0,1.62,6.96,13.53],"f":[0,1.44,6.33,12.33]},{"t":["Here are common muscular disorders.","A muscle strain is overstretching or tearing of muscle fibres.","Tendinitis is inflammation of a tendon.","Myositis is inflammation of muscle tissue.","Muscular dystrophies are a group of genetic disorders characterized by progressive muscle degeneration and weakness.","Fibromyalgia is chronic, widespread pain and tenderness.","And rhabdomyolysis is severe muscle breakdown, which releases muscle contents into the blood and can damage the kidneys."],"s":[0,2.17,6.52,9.13,11.74,18.26,21.3],"f":[0,2.11,6.32,8.84,11.37,17.68,20.63]},{"t":["These clinical terms describe muscle problems.","Myalgia is muscle pain, and myopathy is disease of muscle.","Atrophy is a decrease in muscle size, and hypertrophy is an increase in muscle size.","A spasm is a sudden involuntary contraction, and a cramp is a painful, sustained spasm.","A contracture is a permanent shortening that limits movement.","And tendinopathy is a disorder of a tendon."],"s":[0,3.35,7.7,13.53,19.6,23.4],"f":[0,2.94,6.9,12.15,17.86,21.27]},{"t":["How do we evaluate muscle injury and disease?","We start with the physical examination: strength, tenderness and range of motion.","Creatine kinase, or C K, is a common blood marker of skeletal muscle injury. Aldolase and myoglobin can be useful in selected cases, while C R P and E S R are nonspecific markers of inflammation, not direct markers of muscle injury.","Electromyography, or E M G, records the electrical activity of muscles.","A muscle biopsy takes a sample of tissue.","Ultrasound gives a real-time view of muscles and tears, and MRI gives a detailed view of the muscle and its surroundings."],"s":[0,3.1,7.76,24.04,28.31,31.41],"f":[0,3.02,7.55,23.41,27.56,30.58]},{"t":["These are the main muscle procedures.","An intramuscular injection delivers medicine into a muscle.","A muscle biopsy takes a small sample for analysis.","Myotomy is surgical cutting of a muscle, and myorrhaphy is suturing of a muscle.","Fasciotomy is an incision of the fascia to relieve dangerous pressure.","Tenotomy is surgical division of a tendon, and tendon repair fixes a torn tendon.","And trigger point therapy and rehabilitation reduce pain and improve function."],"s":[0,2.65,6.64,10.3,15.5,20.26,25.98],"f":[0,2.29,5.98,9.45,14.29,18.62,23.88]},{"t":["Finally, the drugs for the muscular system.","Analgesics such as paracetamol relieve pain.","NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and diclofenac reduce pain and inflammation.","Some centrally acting muscle relaxants, such as cyclobenzaprine, may be used short term for selected painful muscle spasms. Antispasticity medicines such as baclofen and tizanidine target spasticity, which is different from ordinary acute muscle spasm.","Corticosteroids such as prednisone suppress inflammation.","Local anesthetics such as lidocaine block nerve conduction in one area.","And botulinum toxin reduces spasticity."],"s":[0,3.34,6.21,10.98,27.69,30.55,35.8],"f":[0,3.12,5.79,10.25,25.85,28.52,33.43]},null,{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. What does chondromalacia mean? A, Cartilage inflammation. B, Cartilage softening. C, Cartilage calcification. D, Cartilage fracture. The answer is B, Cartilage softening.","Question 7. The combining form spondyl o means: A, Bone. B, Vertebra. C, Cartilage. D, Ligament. The answer is B, Vertebra.","Question 8. What does osteopenia mean? A, Increased density. B, Normal density. C, Decreased density. D, Bone infection. The answer is C, Decreased density.","Question 9. The combining form my o means: A, Muscle. B, Bone. C, Joint. D, Marrow. The answer is A, Muscle.","Question 10. The term cost o refers to the: A, Skull. B, Vertebra. C, Ribs. D, Pelvis. The answer is C, Ribs."],"s":[0,2.6,20.41,35.14,51.83,65.23],"f":[0,2.45,19.02,32.38,47.81,60.33]},null,{"t":["Check yourself, questions 16 to 20.","Question 16. Which bone forms the upper arm? A, Humerus. B, Ulna. C, Radius. D, Clavicle. The answer is A, Humerus.","Question 17. Which type of bone is the patella? A, Long bone. B, Irregular bone. C, Flat bone. D, Sesamoid bone. The answer is D, Sesamoid bone.","Question 18. Where is red marrow mainly found in adults? A, Spongy bone of flat bones. B, Articular cartilage. C, Long-bone diaphysis. D, Compact bone. The answer is A, Spongy bone of flat bones.","Question 19. Which bone is NOT appendicular? A, Scapula. B, Femur. C, Rib. D, Hip bone. The answer is C, Rib.","Question 20. Which structure is the site of blood cell formation? A, Bone marrow. B, Articular cartilage. C, Periosteum. D, Ligament. The answer is A, Bone marrow."],"s":[0,2.99,17.6,34.14,53.56,68.05],"f":[0,2.71,16.38,31.69,49.65,62.99]},null,null,null,null],"4":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture four.","Today we study two systems that work very closely together: the cardiovascular system, which is the heart and the blood vessels, and the respiratory system, which is the airways and the lungs.","One system brings in the oxygen, and the other carries it to every cell in the body.","Let us begin with the heart."],"s":[0,1.92,13.42,18.63],"f":[0,1.77,12.34,17.09]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","First, use the word parts of these two systems: the roots, prefixes and suffixes.","Second, describe the main parts of the heart, the vessels, the airways and the lungs.","Third, recognise the common signs and disorders of the heart, vessels and lungs.","And fourth, know the main tests, procedures and drug classes.","At the end, you will test yourself with fifty multiple-choice questions."],"s":[0,3.71,8.91,13.91,19.17,23.2],"f":[0,3.37,8.33,12.85,17.65,21.31]},{"t":["Here are the cardiovascular roots.","Cardio means heart, as in cardiomegaly, an enlarged heart.","Arterio means artery, as in arteriosclerosis, hardening of an artery.","Veno and phlebo both mean vein, as in venous thrombosis and phlebitis.","Vaso and angio both mean vessel, as in vasodilation and angiography.","Aorto is the aorta, as in aortitis.","Hemo means blood, as in hemorrhage.","And corono refers to the coronary vessels, the vessels that supply the heart itself, as in coronary artery."],"s":[0,2.65,6.79,11.97,17.04,22.25,25.59,28.09],"f":[0,2.15,6.03,10.69,15.46,20.26,23.27,25.7]},{"t":["Now the prefixes and suffixes we use most often with the heart and vessels.","Brady means slow, as in bradycardia, and tachy means fast, as in tachycardia.","Hyper means high, as in hypertension, and hypo means low, as in hypotension.","Dys means abnormal, as in dysrhythmia, and anti means against, as in anticoagulant.","For suffixes: itis is inflammation, as in arteritis; osis is an abnormal condition, as in atherosclerosis; emia is a blood condition, as in anemia; pathy is disease, as in cardiomyopathy; ectomy is surgical removal, as in endarterectomy; and plasty is surgical repair, as in angioplasty."],"s":[0,4.84,10.31,15.71,21.71],"f":[0,4.53,9.47,14.43,19.73]},{"t":["Let us put these parts together.","Hypertension is high blood pressure.","Bradycardia is a slow heart rate.","Atherosclerosis is plaque buildup in the arterial intima that may narrow the arteries.","Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein.","Cardiomyopathy is disease of the heart muscle: cardio, heart, myo, muscle, and pathy, disease.","Angioplasty is catheter-based widening of a narrowed blood vessel.","Thrombosis is a blood clot in a vessel.","And aortectomy is surgical removal of all or part of the aorta, an uncommon and major procedure.","Try reading each one using the suffix first."],"s":[0,2.49,4.57,7.06,12.46,14.95,20.76,24.5,27.82,34.88],"f":[0,2.34,4.28,6.62,11.68,14.02,19.47,22.97,26.09,32.71]},{"t":["This slide shows the anatomy of the heart in three views.","On the left is the external view.","Notice the superior and inferior vena cava bringing blood back to the right atrium, the aorta and the pulmonary trunk leaving the heart, and the apex at the bottom.","In the middle, the heart is opened to show the four chambers: two atria above and two ventricles below.","The interatrial and interventricular septa separate the right side from the left.","Between the chambers are the valves: the tricuspid valve on the right and the mitral, or bicuspid, valve on the left, plus the pulmonary and aortic semilunar valves at the exits.","On the right is the coronary circulation, the heart's own blood supply.","The right coronary artery and the left coronary artery, with its circumflex and anterior interventricular branches, feed the heart muscle, and the cardiac veins drain into the coronary sinus."],"s":[0,3.83,6.24,16.38,22.75,27.87,38.86,43.42],"f":[0,3.44,5.6,14.99,20.91,25.62,35.72,39.8]},{"t":["There are three types of blood vessel.","Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","They generally have thicker walls and higher pressures than veins; the aortic and pulmonary valves are heart valves, not valves within arteries.","Veins carry blood toward the heart.","Many veins, especially in the limbs, contain valves that help prevent backflow; not all veins have valves.","Capillaries are the tiny vessels between them, with a wall only one cell thick, and this is where gases, nutrients and wastes are exchanged.","A simple trick: artery and away both begin with the letter a."],"s":[0,2.59,5.18,13.33,15.55,21.84,30.73],"f":[0,2.52,5.04,12.95,15.11,21.22,29.86]},{"t":["Large arteries and veins have three principal wall layers surrounding a lumen; capillaries are an important exception.","The tunica intima is the inner layer.","The tunica media contains smooth muscle and elastic tissue; its thickness varies by vessel type.","And the tunica externa, also called the adventitia, is the outer layer.","Now compare the three vessels in the table.","Arteries have a narrow lumen and a thick wall.","Veins have a wide lumen, a thin wall, and valves.","Capillaries have a very narrow lumen and a wall just one cell thick."],"s":[0,6.36,8.98,14.59,19.08,22.07,25.43,29.17],"f":[0,6.14,8.67,14.09,18.43,21.32,24.57,28.19]},{"t":["Blood travels in two circuits.","The pulmonary circulation takes oxygen-poor blood from the heart to the lungs, where it picks up oxygen, and then returns oxygen-rich blood to the heart.","The systemic circulation takes that oxygen-rich blood from the heart to the whole body, and then returns oxygen-poor blood back to the heart.","Remember: pulmonary means lung, so the pulmonary circuit is the lung circuit."],"s":[0,2.27,11.81,20.3],"f":[0,2.04,10.62,18.44]},{"t":["This slide shows the major arteries.","Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","Systemic arteries carry oxygen-rich blood, while the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the heart to the lungs.","Follow the aorta: it begins as the ascending aorta, curves as the aortic arch, and then runs down as the thoracic aorta and the abdominal aorta, before dividing into the common iliac arteries.","The coronary arteries, the first branches of the aorta, supply the heart muscle itself.","From the aortic arch come the common carotid arteries to the head and neck and the subclavian arteries to the arms; on the right side, both arise from a short common trunk, the brachiocephalic trunk.","In the arm, follow the brachial artery, which divides into the radial and ulnar arteries.","In the leg, follow the femoral artery, then the popliteal artery behind the knee, then the anterior and posterior tibial arteries, and finally the dorsalis pedis artery on the top of the foot.","Doctors feel many of these arteries to check the pulse."],"s":[0,2.63,5.21,13.18,26.61,32.36,45.06,50.9,62.9],"f":[0,2.4,4.71,11.98,24.32,29.29,40.28,45.5,56.24]},{"t":["This slide shows the major veins.","Veins return blood to the heart.","Systemic veins carry oxygen-poor blood, but the pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the heart.","The two largest veins are the superior vena cava, which drains the upper body, and the inferior vena cava, which drains the lower body.","In the neck are the jugular veins, and in the arm are the subclavian, axillary, cephalic and basilic veins.","The median cubital vein, at the front of the elbow, is the vein we most often use to take blood.","The coronary sinus drains blood from the heart muscle itself.","In the abdomen, look at the hepatic portal vein, which carries blood from the gut to the liver, and the hepatic and renal veins.","In the leg are the femoral vein, the great saphenous vein, which is the longest vein in the body, the popliteal vein and the tibial veins."],"s":[0,2.49,4.8,12.4,21.07,28.04,33.97,37.92,45.37],"f":[0,2.19,4.33,11.35,19.25,25.39,30.98,34.59,41.31]},{"t":["Now the vital signs, the basic measurements of cardiovascular health that we take for every patient.","The pulse is the rhythmic expansion of an artery with each heartbeat.","The heart rate is the number of beats per minute, and a normal heart rate in an adult is sixty to one hundred beats per minute.","A blood pressure near one hundred and twenty over eighty millimetres of mercury is a familiar reference value; diagnostic thresholds vary by guideline and clinical context.","A normal respiratory rate is twelve to twenty breaths per minute.","Peripheral skin temperature and capillary refill help assess peripheral circulation; a cold limb or delayed refill can be abnormal.","And oxygen saturation, S P O two, is the oxygen bound to hemoglobin.","At sea level, oxygen saturation in most healthy adults is approximately ninety-five to one hundred percent; interpretation depends on clinical context and altitude.","Learn these normal values well, because the clinical terms on the next slide are defined by them."],"s":[0,5.78,10.11,19.5,28.89,32.86,39.72,44.42,52.72],"f":[0,5.51,9.64,18.59,27.54,31.33,37.87,42.35,50.27]},{"t":["These clinical terms describe cardiovascular findings.","According to the 2025 American Heart Association and American College of Cardiology guideline, hypertension begins at an average systolic pressure of one hundred and thirty or diastolic pressure of eighty millimetres of mercury, based on repeated accurate measurements. Other guidelines use different thresholds.","Hypotension is low blood pressure, below ninety over sixty.","Tachycardia is a fast heart rate, above one hundred beats per minute.","Bradycardia is a slow heart rate, below sixty.","Arrhythmia is an irregular heart rhythm.","A murmur is a sound produced by turbulent blood flow and may be physiological or pathological.","Edema is swelling caused by fluid build-up.","And cyanosis is a bluish colour of the skin caused by low oxygen.","Notice how cyan means blue."],"s":[0,2.46,20.07,23.76,28.67,31.95,34.41,40.96,43.83,49.16],"f":[0,2.37,19.34,22.89,27.62,30.78,33.15,39.46,42.22,47.35]},{"t":["Here are other common signs, the symptoms patients often describe.","Chest pain is discomfort or pressure in the chest.","Dyspnea is the subjective experience of breathing discomfort, often described as shortness of breath.","Fatigue is unusual tiredness.","Palpitations are the feeling of rapid or irregular heartbeats.","Syncope means fainting.","And claudication is leg pain on walking, caused by poor blood flow to the leg muscles, which goes away with rest."],"s":[0,3.97,7.54,13.09,14.68,18.25,19.44],"f":[0,3.92,7.46,12.95,14.52,18.05,19.23]},{"t":["Now the common disorders of the blood vessels.","In atherosclerosis, fatty plaque builds up and narrows the arteries.","An aneurysm is a local bulging of a vessel wall.","Hypertension is chronically high blood pressure.","Peripheral arterial disease means poor blood flow to the limbs.","Deep vein thrombosis, or D V T, is a clot in a deep vein, usually in the leg.","And varicose veins are dilated, twisted veins near the surface, often seen in the legs."],"s":[0,3.15,7.87,11.58,14.7,19,24.75],"f":[0,2.7,6.79,10.03,12.96,16.74,21.95]},{"t":["These are the common disorders of the heart itself.","Coronary artery disease means the coronary arteries are narrowed.","Myocardial infarction is a heart attack: myo means muscle, cardi means heart, and infarction means tissue death, because blocked blood flow injures the heart muscle.","Heart failure means the heart pumps weakly, so fluid backs up and causes congestion.","Arrhythmia is an abnormal heart rhythm.","Valvular heart disease means the valves are narrowed or leaking.","And cardiomyopathy is disease of the heart muscle."],"s":[0,3.26,7.86,18.18,23.6,26.4,30.53],"f":[0,2.88,6.94,16.38,21.36,23.89,27.55]},{"t":["How do we examine the heart and vessels?","The E C G, or E K G, records the electrical activity of the heart.","Echocardiography uses ultrasound to show the chambers, the valves and how well the heart works.","A chest X-ray shows the heart size and the lungs.","Cardiac C T shows the coronary arteries and other structures, and cardiac M R I gives detailed images of structure and function.","Coronary angiography uses contrast dye to find a blockage in the coronary arteries."],"s":[0,2.62,8.03,14.12,17.53,26.58],"f":[0,2.4,7.18,12.67,15.71,24.17]},{"t":["More tests.","A cardiac stress test evaluates the heart under physical exercise or pharmacological stress.","Cardiac troponin indicates myocardial injury but a raised value alone does not establish myocardial infarction. C K M B has a limited contemporary role. B N P and N T pro B N P can support evaluation of heart failure.","As a chemical pathologist, I can tell you that serial high-sensitivity troponin testing, combined with symptoms and ECG findings, is central to evaluating possible acute myocardial infarction.","Finally, pulse oximetry measures the oxygen saturation, and it is used for continuous monitoring."],"s":[0,0.86,6.42,23.52,35.07],"f":[0,0.8,5.98,21.92,32.69]},{"t":["These procedures are used to open and repair vessels.","In cardiac catheterization, a thin tube called a catheter is passed into the vessels to diagnose or treat.","In coronary angiography, contrast dye shows the coronary blockages.","Angioplasty widens a narrowed vessel, often with a balloon. P T C A specifically means percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty.","A stent is a small mesh tube that keeps the artery open.","C A B G, or coronary artery bypass grafting, uses a graft to reroute blood around a blockage.","And valve repair or replacement fixes a damaged heart valve."],"s":[0,3.64,10.91,14.54,22.22,27.07,34.34],"f":[0,3.47,10.4,13.87,21.18,25.81,32.74]},{"t":["Now the drugs for blood pressure.","Antihypertensives is the general name for drugs that lower blood pressure: anti, against, hyper, high, tension, pressure.","Beta blockers lower the heart rate and the blood pressure.","Calcium channel blockers relax the blood vessels.","A C E inhibitors reduce the making of angiotensin two, so the vessels widen.","A R Bs block the angiotensin two receptors.","And diuretics help the kidneys remove sodium and water."],"s":[0,2.18,9.8,13.36,16.77,22.42,26.21],"f":[0,2.06,9.55,12.76,16.02,20.87,24.25]},{"t":["Here are other cardiovascular drug classes.","Nitrates cause vasodilation, widening of the vessels, and relieve angina, which is chest pain from poor blood flow to the heart.","Antianginals lower the heart's oxygen demand and prevent angina.","Antiarrhythmics restore a normal rhythm.","Inotropes increase contractility, the force of contraction, and the cardiac output.","For thrombosis prevention and treatment, anticoagulants inhibit coagulation, antiplatelet medicines reduce platelet activation or aggregation, and thrombolytics can dissolve selected established clots.","Finally, lipid-lowering drugs lower L D L cholesterol.","Notice how the name of each class tells you what it does."],"s":[0,2.71,12.21,16.28,18.54,23.52,33.47,37.09],"f":[0,2.6,11.72,15.62,17.79,22.57,32.12,35.59]},{"t":["We now move to the respiratory system.","The airway is divided into two parts.","The upper tract is the nose, the nasal cavity, the sinuses, the pharynx and the larynx.","It filters, warms and humidifies the air, and it gives us our voice and our sense of smell.","The lower tract is the trachea, the bronchi, the bronchioles and the lungs.","It conducts air to the lungs, it is where gas exchange happens, and it helps keep the acid base balance of the blood."],"s":[0,2.64,5.49,11.3,17.07,21.94],"f":[0,2.31,4.98,10.22,15.62,19.77]},{"t":["Here are the respiratory roots.","Rhino and naso both mean nose, as in rhinitis and nasal.","Sinuso means a sinus, a cavity, as in sinusitis.","Pharyngo is the pharynx, or throat, as in pharyngitis, and laryngo is the larynx, or voice box, as in laryngitis.","Tracheo is the trachea, as in tracheostomy.","Broncho is the bronchus, as in bronchitis.","Pneumo can refer to air or the lung, while pulmono refers to the lung; pneumonia is infection and inflammation of lung tissue.","Pleuro is the pleura, the lining of the lung, as in pleuritis.","Spiro means to breathe, as in spirometer.","And oxo means oxygen, as in oximetry."],"s":[0,2.08,6.64,10.38,18.68,21.59,24.49,33.63,38.61,41.51],"f":[0,1.9,6.1,9.52,17.14,19.81,22.48,30.86,35.43,38.1]},{"t":["Now the respiratory prefixes and suffixes.","Tachy means fast, as in tachypnea, and brady means slow, as in bradypnea.","Dys means difficult, as in dyspnea, and eu means normal, as in eupnea.","Hyper means excessive and hypo means deficient, as in hyperoxia and hypoxia.","Anti means against, as in antitussive, a drug against cough.","For suffixes: pnea means breathing, as in apnea; oxia is an oxygen condition, as in hypoxia; and capnia is a carbon dioxide condition, as in hypercapnia.","Then itis, oma, ectomy, as in lobectomy, scopy, as in bronchoscopy, and graphy, a recording."],"s":[0,3.16,8.3,13.18,18.82,23,34.29],"f":[0,2.83,7.61,12.22,17.41,21.26,32.26]},{"t":["Let us read some respiratory terms.","Dyspnea is difficult breathing.","Tachypnea is rapid breathing.","Apnea is cessation of airflow or breathing; it may be brief or prolonged.","Hypoxia is low oxygen in the tissues.","Bronchoscopy is looking inside the bronchi.","Rhinitis is inflamed nasal lining, and laryngitis is an inflamed larynx.","Pneumothorax is air in the pleural space: pneumo, air, and thorax, chest.","Tracheostomy is an opening into the trachea.","Pleuritis is inflammation of the pleura, often causing pain with breathing."],"s":[0,2.63,4.38,6.13,11.81,14.88,17.5,22.31,27.57,30.63],"f":[0,2.47,4.12,5.76,11.12,14,16.47,21,25.94,28.82]},{"t":["This slide shows the nose and nasal cavity in a sagittal section.","The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into right and left sides.","On the side wall are three shelves of bone called the superior, middle and inferior nasal conchae.","They make the air swirl, so it is warmed and cleaned.","Below is the palate, the hard palate in front, made of bone, and the soft palate behind, made of muscle.","There are four groups of paired paranasal sinuses: frontal, ethmoidal air cells, sphenoidal and maxillary.","Together, the nose and sinuses filter, warm and humidify the air, and give us the sense of smell."],"s":[0,4.43,8.86,15.13,19.19,26.57,32.11],"f":[0,4.22,8.44,14.42,18.29,25.33,30.61]},{"t":["These are the air passages from the throat to the lungs.","The pharynx, or throat, has three parts: the nasopharynx behind the nose, the oropharynx behind the mouth, and the laryngopharynx just above the larynx.","The larynx, or voice box, is built from cartilages.","The epiglottis is the lid that closes over the airway when we swallow.","The thyroid cartilage forms the Adam's apple, and below it is the cricoid cartilage.","Inside are the vocal cords, the true vocal folds.","Then the trachea divides into the right and left primary bronchi, which divide into lobar bronchi, then the bronchioles, and finally end in the alveoli, the tiny air sacs where gas exchange happens."],"s":[0,3.59,13.51,17.14,21.69,27.25,30.89],"f":[0,3.17,11.84,15.24,19.35,24.29,27.38]},{"t":["This slide shows the lungs and their lobes.","Remember this key fact: the right lung has three lobes, the superior, middle and inferior lobes, separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures.","The left lung has only two lobes, the superior and inferior lobes, separated by the oblique fissure, because it leaves room for the heart.","Each lung is covered by the pleura.","The visceral pleura covers the lung, the parietal pleura lines the chest wall, and between them is the pleural cavity, which contains a thin layer of fluid."],"s":[0,2.77,11.93,20.36,22.75],"f":[0,2.47,10.84,18.47,20.67]},{"t":["Here are the common respiratory signs.","Cough is the forceful expulsion of air, and sputum is the mucus that is coughed up.","Dyspnea is shortness of breath.","For a resting adult, tachypnea commonly refers to a respiratory rate above twenty per minute, while bradypnea is an unusually slow respiratory rate; thresholds vary with age and clinical state.","Wheezing is a whistling sound from narrow airways, and stridor is a high-pitched sound from the upper airway.","Cyanosis is bluish lips and nails.","Chest retractions mean the chest wall is pulled in.","Clubbing is bulbous enlargement of the distal fingers with changes in nail curvature and the nail-bed angle.","Hemoptysis is coughing up blood.","And orthopnea is breathlessness when lying flat: ortho means straight or upright, because the patient breathes better sitting up."],"s":[0,2.27,8.32,10.21,21.56,28.37,30.64,34.04,40.47,42.36],"f":[0,2.25,8.25,10.12,21.37,28.11,30.36,33.74,40.11,41.98]},{"t":["These clinical terms describe respiratory conditions and findings.","Apnea is an episode of absent breathing or airflow, with variable duration.","Hypoxia is low oxygen in the tissues; low oxygen in the arterial blood is called hypoxemia.","Hypercapnia is high carbon dioxide in the blood.","Pleurisy, also called pleuritis, is painful inflammation of the pleura.","Rhinitis, sinusitis, pharyngitis, laryngitis and bronchitis are inflammation of the nasal lining, the sinuses, the pharynx, the larynx and the bronchi; pharyngitis causes a sore throat.","Pneumonia is a lung infection.","Asthma is a chronic inflammatory airway disease with variable respiratory symptoms and variable expiratory airflow limitation.","And COPD, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, is a long-term limitation of airflow."],"s":[0,3.67,9.17,16.51,20.18,24.77,36.69,38.99,46.32],"f":[0,3.48,8.7,15.66,19.14,23.49,34.79,36.97,43.93]},{"t":["These terms are used to assess breathing.","Respiratory rate is measured in breaths per minute. Around twelve to twenty at rest is a common adult reference interval, not a universal range for children or illness.","Oxygen saturation, or S P O two, is commonly about ninety-five to one hundred percent in healthy adults at sea level, but targets and expected values depend on the patient and environment.","Auscultation means listening to the chest with a stethoscope.","When we listen, we may hear crackles, also called rales, which are popping sounds that may occur with fluid or the sudden opening of small airways.","Or we may hear rhonchi, which are low-pitched sounds caused by mucus in the airways."],"s":[0,2.48,12.4,23.74,26.92,36.13],"f":[0,2.42,12.08,23.12,26.23,35.2]},{"t":["Now the disorders of the lower respiratory tract: the bronchi, the lungs and the pleura.","Acute bronchitis is inflammation of the bronchi with cough and mucus.","Pneumonia is an infection of the alveoli.","Bronchiolitis is inflammation of the bronchioles, and it is common in young children.","Tuberculosis is a chronic infection of the lungs.","Pleuritis is inflammation of the pleura, which causes chest pain.","And a lung abscess is a pus-filled cavity in the lung."],"s":[0,5.4,10.46,13.41,18.81,22.36,26.81],"f":[0,4.84,9.15,11.82,16.68,20.05,24.03]},{"t":["And the disorders of the upper respiratory tract: the nose, the sinuses and the throat.","The common cold is a viral infection of the upper airway.","Rhinitis is inflamed nasal lining.","Sinusitis is inflammation of the paranasal sinuses.","Pharyngitis is an inflamed pharynx, and tonsillitis is inflamed tonsils.","Laryngitis is an inflamed larynx, and it causes hoarseness of the voice.","Notice that most of these words are simply a root plus itis."],"s":[0,5.27,9.24,12.12,16.07,21.75,26.7],"f":[0,4.77,8.33,10.9,14.73,19.59,24.09]},{"t":["How do we examine the airways and lungs?","The chest X-ray is the basic lung image, and the chest C T scan gives detailed cross-sections.","Spirometry measures airflow and exhaled volumes such as F E V one and forced vital capacity. It cannot measure residual volume or total lung capacity by itself.","Pulse oximetry measures the oxygen saturation.","The arterial blood gas measures oxygen, carbon dioxide and the p H of arterial blood.","A sputum exam looks for infection and abnormal cells.","Bronchoscopy looks directly at the airways.","And a sleep study detects breathing disorders during sleep."],"s":[0,3.22,10.08,20.96,23.38,29.42,33.05,35.47],"f":[0,2.97,9.28,19.3,21.52,27.09,30.43,32.66]},{"t":["Here are the main groups of respiratory drugs.","Bronchodilators open the airways.","Inhaled corticosteroids reduce airway inflammation.","Mucolytics and expectorants thin and clear the mucus.","Antihistamines and decongestants relieve allergy and congestion.","Antitussives suppress cough.","Antibiotics treat bacterial infection.","Leukotriene modifiers reduce inflammation and bronchospasm.","And oxygen therapy improves oxygenation.","Again, the name tells you the job: broncho dilator opens the bronchi."],"s":[0,3.14,5.71,9.54,13.19,17.76,19.82,22.62,27.2,30.55],"f":[0,2.77,4.99,8.56,11.97,16.38,18.25,20.87,25.03,28.13]},{"t":["Finally, the procedures on the airways and pleura.","Bronchoscopy gives an endoscopic view of the bronchi.","Endotracheal intubation places a tube inside the trachea to secure the airway.","A tracheostomy is a surgical opening into the trachea.","Nebulization delivers a drug as an aerosol, a fine mist, to the airways.","Thoracentesis uses a needle to remove fluid from the pleural space.","A chest tube drains air or fluid from the pleura.","Sputum collection gives a sample for the laboratory.","Pulmonary function testing includes spirometry and, when indicated, lung volume measurements and diffusing capacity tests.","That completes the content of this lecture."],"s":[0,3.21,6.42,11.23,14.84,20.05,24.47,28.48,31.68,37.7],"f":[0,3.09,6.17,10.8,14.28,19.29,23.54,27.4,30.49,36.27]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 1 to 5.","Question 1. Inflammation of a blood vessel? A, Vasoconstriction. B, Thrombosis. C, Vasculitis. D, Atherosclerosis. The answer is C, Vasculitis.","Question 2. Abnormal heart rhythm? A, Bradycardia. B, Arrhythmia. C, Murmur. D, Tachycardia. The answer is B, Arrhythmia.","Question 3. Surgical repair of a blood vessel? A, Angioplasty. B, Arteriectomy. C, Vasectomy. D, Phlebotomy. The answer is A, Angioplasty.","Question 4. Persistently elevated blood pressure? A, Hypotension. B, Hypertension. C, Orthostatic hypotension. D, Shock. The answer is B, Hypertension.","Question 5. Formation of a clot in a vessel? A, Embolism. B, Thrombosis. C, Hemorrhage. D, Aneurysm. The answer is B, Thrombosis."],"s":[0,2.6,19.19,33.98,50.28,67.23],"f":[0,2.53,17.96,31.58,46.62,62.37]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. Escape of blood from a vessel? A, Hematoma. B, Hemorrhage. C, Hematopoiesis. D, Hemostasis. The answer is B, Hemorrhage.","Question 7. Abnormal blowing heart sound? A, Murmur. B, Gallop. C, Click. D, Rub. The answer is A, Murmur.","Question 8. Partial or complete blockage of a vessel? A, Dilatation. B, Permeability. C, Perfusion. D, Occlusion. The answer is D, Occlusion.","Question 9. Blood collected outside the vessels? A, Ecchymosis. B, Petechiae. C, Hematoma. D, Purpura. The answer is C, Hematoma.","Question 10. Cessation of bleeding? A, Hemostasis. B, Coagulation. C, Fibrinolysis. D, Thrombolysis. The answer is A, Hemostasis."],"s":[0,2.6,18.3,31.99,47.9,63.42],"f":[0,2.45,16.93,29.8,44.61,58.76]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 11 to 15.","Question 11. Narrowing of an artery? A, Aneurysm. B, Arteriosclerosis. C, Stenosis. D, Vasodilation. The answer is C, Stenosis.","Question 12. Widening of a blood vessel? A, Vasoconstriction. B, Vasodilation. C, Vasospasm. D, Thrombophlebitis. The answer is B, Vasodilation.","Question 13. Heart muscle disease? A, Myocarditis. B, Endocarditis. C, Pericarditis. D, Cardiomyopathy. The answer is D, Cardiomyopathy.","Question 14. Surgical removal of the aorta? A, Angiectomy. B, Aortitis. C, Aortectomy. D, Aortoplasty. The answer is C, Aortectomy.","Question 15. Inadequate blood supply to tissues? A, Ischemia. B, Hyperemia. C, Anemia. D, Infarction. The answer is A, Ischemia."],"s":[0,3.14,19.01,35.46,52.22,68.63],"f":[0,2.68,17.35,32.63,48.25,63.52]},{"t":["Let us go through questions sixteen to twenty.","Try to answer each one before I give the answer.","Question sixteen.","Displacement of a blood clot.","The answer is A, Embolism.","Question seventeen.","Procedure that holds a narrowed artery open with a mesh tube.","The answer is B, Stent placement.","Question eighteen.","Inflammation of the pericardium.","The answer is A, Pericarditis.","Question nineteen.","Clot in a vein with inflammation.","The answer is B, Thrombophlebitis.","Question twenty.","Bursting of an aneurysm.","The answer is B, Rupture."],"s":[0,3.04,6.31,7.96,10.24,13.15,14.89,18.9,21.98,23.63,26.11,29.29,30.91,33.26,36.58,38.12,40.15],"f":[0,2.82,5.87,7.39,9.47,12.01,13.6,17.54,20.35,21.83,24.24,27.09,28.6,30.75,33.7,35.15,36.96]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 21 to 25.","Question 21. Chamber that pumps blood into the aorta? A, Right atrium. B, Right ventricle. C, Left atrium. D, Left ventricle. The answer is D, Left ventricle.","Question 22. Valve between the right atrium (RA) and right ventricle (RV)? A, Mitral. B, Tricuspid. C, Aortic. D, Pulmonary. The answer is B, Tricuspid.","Question 23. Artery to the front wall of the left ventricle (LV)? A, Right coronary. B, LAD (left anterior descending). C, Circumflex. D, Marginal. The answer is B, LAD (left anterior descending).","Question 24. ECG wave of ventricular depolarization? A, P wave. B, QRS complex. C, T wave. D, U wave. The answer is B, QRS complex.","Question 25. Phase in which the aortic valve is open (ejection)? A, Atrial systole. B, Isovolumetric contraction. C, Ventricular systole. D, Isovolumetric relaxation. The answer is C, Ventricular systole."],"s":[0,3.23,20.13,37.47,57.27,75.33],"f":[0,3.05,18.8,34.84,52.78,69.28]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 26 to 30.","Question 26. The heart's natural pacemaker? A, AV node. B, Bundle of His. C, Purkinje fibers. D, SA node. The answer is D, SA node.","Question 27. Thinnest vessels; site of exchange? A, Arteries. B, Veins. C, Capillaries. D, Venules. The answer is C, Capillaries.","Question 28. Main function of the pulmonary circuit? A, Oxygen-rich blood to body. B, Blood to the kidneys. C, Return blood from body. D, Remove carbon dioxide, oxygenate blood. The answer is D, Remove carbon dioxide, oxygenate blood.","Question 29. Blood pressure of 140 over 90 millimeters of mercury is: A, Normal. B, Hypotension. C, Hypertension (stage 2, ACC/AHA). D, Severe hypotension. The answer is C, Hypertension (stage 2, ACC/AHA).","Question 30. Drug that inhibits clot formation? A, Nitrates. B, Beta-blockers. C, Anticoagulants. D, ACE inhibitors. The answer is C, Anticoagulants."],"s":[0,2.98,19,34.35,56.28,79.27],"f":[0,2.73,17.55,32.02,52.29,73.24]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 31 to 35.","Question 31. What does dyspnea mean? A, Difficult breathing. B, Slow breathing. C, Absence of breathing. D, Coughing blood. The answer is A, Difficult breathing.","Question 32. The term for rapid breathing is: A, Bradypnea. B, Tachypnea. C, Apnea. D, Orthopnea. The answer is B, Tachypnea.","Question 33. What does rhin o mean? A, Lung. B, Throat. C, Pleura. D, Nose. The answer is D, Nose.","Question 34. Inflammation of the bronchi is: A, Bronchitis. B, Rhinitis. C, Laryngitis. D, Sinusitis. The answer is A, Bronchitis.","Question 35. What does hypoxia mean? A, High carbon dioxide. B, Chest pain. C, Low oxygen level. D, Cough. The answer is C, Low oxygen level."],"s":[0,3.25,19.52,34.3,48.09,63.93],"f":[0,3.04,17.95,31.66,44.53,58.93]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 36 to 40.","Question 36. The term for absence of breathing is: A, Eupnea. B, Tachypnea. C, Dyspnea. D, Apnea. The answer is D, Apnea.","Question 37. What does pleuritis mean? A, Lung infection. B, Inflammation of pleura. C, Nose inflammation. D, Bronchial narrowing. The answer is B, Inflammation of pleura.","Question 38. The combining form pneum o means: A, Lung or air. B, Blood. C, Heart. D, Muscle. The answer is A, Lung or air.","Question 39. What does hemoptysis mean? A, Blue skin. B, Wheezing. C, Sputum production. D, Coughing blood. The answer is D, Coughing blood.","Question 40. The term for normal breathing is: A, Apnea. B, Bradypnea. C, Eupnea. D, Hypercapnia. The answer is C, Eupnea."],"s":[0,3.01,17.62,35.24,49.99,65.77],"f":[0,2.72,16.25,32.56,45.9,60.5]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 41 to 45.","Question 41. Main muscle of inspiration? A, Diaphragm. B, Trachea. C, Epiglottis. D, Pleura. The answer is A, Diaphragm.","Question 42. Gas exchange mainly occurs in the: A, Trachea. B, Larynx. C, Nasal cavity. D, Alveoli. The answer is D, Alveoli.","Question 43. During inspiration, the diaphragm: A, Relaxes, moves up. B, Closes the glottis. C, Contracts, moves down. D, Stops airflow. The answer is C, Contracts, moves down.","Question 44. The right lung usually has: A, 2 lobes. B, 1 lobe. C, 4 lobes. D, 3 lobes. The answer is D, 3 lobes.","Question 45. The left lung usually has: A, 2 lobes. B, 3 lobes. C, 4 lobes. D, 1 lobe. The answer is A, 2 lobes."],"s":[0,3.36,18.45,33.41,50.91,65.89],"f":[0,3.07,16.99,30.83,47.09,60.63]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 46 to 50.","Question 46. The trachea divides into the: A, Lobar bronchi. B, Terminal bronchioles. C, Alveolar ducts. D, Right and left main bronchi. The answer is D, Right and left main bronchi.","Question 47. Oxygen is carried mainly bound to: A, Myoglobin. B, Albumin. C, Hemoglobin. D, Transferrin. The answer is C, Hemoglobin.","Question 48. Most carbon dioxide is transported as: A, Bicarbonate. B, Carbaminohemoglobin. C, Dissolved carbon dioxide in plasma. D, Carboxyhemoglobin. The answer is A, Bicarbonate.","Question 49. The pleura mainly helps to: A, Warm and humidify inhaled air. B, Exchange gases with blood. C, Produce surfactant. D, Reduce friction during breathing. The answer is D, Reduce friction during breathing.","Question 50. Spirometry mainly assesses: A, Airflow and lung volumes. B, Oxygen saturation of blood. C, Arterial blood pH and gases. D, Lung structure on imaging. The answer is A, Airflow and lung volumes."],"s":[0,3.04,21.16,36.74,55.7,74.59],"f":[0,2.77,19.15,33.6,51.03,68.49]}],"5":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture five.","Today we study two systems that deal with what we take in and what we get rid of: the gastrointestinal system, which digests our food, and the urinary system, which cleans our blood and makes urine.","We will look at the digestive organs, the kidneys and the urinary tract, and learn the words that describe them."],"s":[0,1.9,13.83],"f":[0,1.84,12.9]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","First, use the word parts of the digestive and urinary systems: the roots, prefixes and suffixes.","Second, name the parts of the G I tract and the urinary system, including the kidney and its working unit, the nephron.","Third, recognise the common signs and disorders of these systems.","And fourth, know the main tests, procedures and drug classes.","At the end, you will test yourself with forty multiple-choice questions."],"s":[0,3.71,10.38,17.67,22.41,26.44],"f":[0,3.36,9.41,16.12,20.13,23.79]},{"t":["The G I system has two parts.","The first is the alimentary canal, one long tube from the mouth to the anus.","Food passes through the oral cavity, the pharynx, the esophagus, the stomach, the small intestine, the large intestine, the rectum, and finally the anus.","The second part is the accessory organs, which help digestion without food passing through them.","The salivary glands make saliva.","The liver makes bile, and the gallbladder stores it.","And the pancreas makes digestive enzymes and bicarbonate."],"s":[0,2.5,7.66,17.6,23.64,26.14,29.07],"f":[0,2.2,6.78,15.73,21.23,23.44,26.22]},{"t":["Here are the G I roots.","Gastro means stomach, as in gastritis.","Entero means intestine, as in enteritis.","Esophago is the esophagus, as in esophagitis.","Hepato means liver, as in hepatomegaly.","Chole means bile or gall, as in cholelithiasis, stones in the gallbladder.","Cholangio is the bile vessel, or bile duct, as in cholangitis.","Pancreato is the pancreas, as in pancreatitis.","Colo is the colon, as in colitis.","Procto is the rectum and anus, as in proctology.","And oro and stomato both mean the mouth, as in stomatitis, inflammation of the mouth."],"s":[0,1.92,4.8,8.14,11.82,14.88,20.12,24.78,28.51,31.22,35.01],"f":[0,1.65,4.2,7.14,10.55,13.32,17.97,22.21,25.51,28.03,31.48]},{"t":["Now the suffixes and prefixes of the digestive system.","Itis is inflammation, as in gastritis, algia is pain, as in gastralgia, and megaly is enlargement, as in hepatomegaly.","Emia is a blood condition, as in hyperbilirubinemia, too much bilirubin in the blood.","Pepsia means digestion, as in dyspepsia, indigestion.","The ending in diarrhea means flow or discharge.","Emesis means vomiting, as in hematemesis, vomiting blood.","Phagia means eating or swallowing, as in dysphagia.","Chezia means defecation, as in hematochezia.","And lithiasis means stone formation, as in cholelithiasis.","For prefixes: dys means difficult, painful or abnormal; hyper, excessive, as in hyperemesis, severe vomiting; hypo, below normal, as in hypoglycemia; peri, around, as in perianal; and intra, within, as in intra-abdominal."],"s":[0,4.56,14.17,21.26,24.81,28.86,32.91,36.96,39.99,44.04],"f":[0,4.38,13.62,20.42,23.83,27.72,31.61,35.5,38.41,42.3]},{"t":["Digestion begins in the mouth.","Here food is taken in, chewed and mixed with saliva.","The tongue moves the food, and at the back you can see the soft palate and the uvula.","An adult has thirty-two permanent teeth: eight incisors for cutting, four canines for tearing, eight premolars, and twelve molars for grinding.","There are three pairs of salivary glands.","The parotid gland is in front of the ear and is the largest.","The submandibular gland is under the jaw.","And the sublingual gland is under the tongue: sub means under, and lingual means tongue."],"s":[0,2.4,5.79,10.7,19.71,22.41,26.28,29.2],"f":[0,2.11,5.17,9.58,17.76,20.21,23.7,26.28]},{"t":["From the esophagus to the rectum, the wall of the G I tract has the same four layers.","The innermost layer is the mucosa, for secretion and absorption.","Next is the submucosa, for support and nourishment, with blood vessels and nerves.","Then the muscularis externa, the muscle layer that moves food along.","And the outer layer is the serosa, or adventitia, for protection and support.","The wave-like movement made by the muscle layer is called peristalsis."],"s":[0,5.57,9.82,15.16,19.6,24.46],"f":[0,5.01,8.89,13.72,17.76,22.49]},{"t":["The esophagus carries swallowed food and liquid from the pharynx to the stomach.","When we swallow, food passes through the oropharynx and the laryngopharynx; it does not normally enter the nasopharynx, because the soft palate closes it, and the epiglottis covers the entrance to the larynx to protect the airway.","The esophagus has two sphincters: the upper esophageal sphincter at the top, and the lower esophageal sphincter at the gastroesophageal junction, which helps stop acid coming back up.","The stomach has four regions: the cardia near the entry, the fundus, which is the upper dome, the body, which is the main part, and the pylorus, the exit that leads into the duodenum."],"s":[0,4.7,18.08,28.21],"f":[0,4.41,16.94,26.43]},{"t":["The small intestine absorbs most nutrients and much of the ingested water. The large intestine absorbs remaining water and electrolytes.","The small intestine has three parts: the duodenum, the jejunum and the ileum.","The large intestine begins with the cecum, with the appendix attached to it.","Then comes the colon: the ascending colon goes up, the transverse colon goes across, the descending colon goes down, and the S-shaped sigmoid colon leads to the rectum and the anal canal."],"s":[0,7.51,12.4,17.28],"f":[0,7.12,11.75,16.38]},{"t":["These are the accessory digestive organs.","The liver produces bile, metabolizes nutrients and detoxifies the blood.","The gallbladder stores the bile, concentrates it, and releases it when we eat.","The pancreas produces digestive enzymes and bicarbonate, and it also controls blood sugar with its hormones, insulin and glucagon.","Notice that bile and pancreatic juice both flow into the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine."],"s":[0,3.12,8.22,12.86,21.52],"f":[0,2.77,7.62,11.68,19.57]},{"t":["Here are the common G I signs, what patients describe and what we see.","Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing.","Nausea is the feeling of being sick, and vomiting is forceful expulsion of gastric contents through the mouth.","Hematemesis is vomiting blood.","Dyspepsia is indigestion.","Diarrhea is loose, watery stools, and constipation is hard, infrequent stools.","Melena is black, tarry stool, usually from bleeding high in the gut, while hematochezia is bright red blood in the stool, usually from bleeding low in the gut.","Jaundice is yellow skin and eyes.","Bloating is a sense of abdominal fullness or pressure, and abdominal pain is another common digestive symptom."],"s":[0,5.59,7.19,14.39,15.98,17.18,21.58,32.77,35.17],"f":[0,5.38,6.92,13.84,15.38,16.54,20.77,31.53,33.84]},{"t":["These are disorders of the upper G I tract, plus one acute emergency.","GERD, gastroesophageal reflux disease, is acid reflux into the esophagus, which causes heartburn.","Gastritis is an inflamed stomach lining.","A peptic ulcer is an ulcer in the stomach or the duodenum.","Gastroenteritis is inflammation of the stomach and intestines, often caused by infection, and may produce vomiting and diarrhea: gastro means stomach, entero means intestine, and itis means inflammation.","And appendicitis is an inflamed appendix, which is a surgical emergency."],"s":[0,5.74,11.49,14.14,19.44,31.82],"f":[0,5.42,10.83,13.33,18.34,30]},{"t":["Now disorders of the bowel, liver, gallbladder and pancreas.","Irritable bowel syndrome causes abdominal pain with changes in bowel habit.","Ulcerative colitis is a chronic inflammation of the colon.","Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver, often caused by a virus.","Gallstones are stones in the gallbladder, called cholelithiasis.","And pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas."],"s":[0,3.93,9.43,13.51,18.22,22.28],"f":[0,3.5,8.5,12.27,16.51,20.08]},{"t":["How do we examine the G I tract?","An abdominal X-ray shows gas, blockage and calcification.","Ultrasound uses sound waves to image the organs, such as the liver and gallbladder.","A C T scan gives cross-sections of the abdomen, and an M R I gives detailed soft-tissue images.","Upper G I endoscopy looks at the esophagus, the stomach and the duodenum.","And colonoscopy looks at the colon and the rectum."],"s":[0,2.39,6.7,12.35,19.26,24.26],"f":[0,2.18,6.07,11.08,17.29,21.86]},{"t":["More tests.","Capsule endoscopy uses a tiny camera in a capsule that the patient swallows, to image the small intestine.","A barium swallow examines the pharynx and esophagus during swallowing using fluoroscopic imaging. A broader upper GI barium study may also assess the stomach and duodenum.","Stool testing looks for infection, blood and other G I disorders.","The liver function tests measure A L T, A S T, A L P, bilirubin and albumin.","Blood lipase and sometimes amylase assist in evaluating acute pancreatic injury; fecal elastase is used to assess exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","As a chemical pathologist, these are tests I work with every day.","And a biopsy examines tissue under the microscope."],"s":[0,0.8,8.03,18.46,22.87,29.69,37.72,42.53],"f":[0,0.77,7.72,17.76,22.01,28.58,36.3,40.94]},{"t":["These are the G I procedures.","Endoscopy is viewing the G I tract with a scope.","E G D, esophago-gastro-duodenoscopy, looks at the esophagus, stomach and duodenum.","Colonoscopy views the whole colon and rectum, and sigmoidoscopy views only the sigmoid colon and rectum.","A biopsy removes a tissue sample for histological examination, while polypectomy removes a polyp.","E R C P, endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography, uses endoscopy and fluoroscopy to image and often treat disorders of the bile and pancreatic ducts.","Paracentesis uses a needle to sample or drain fluid from the peritoneal cavity.","An N G tube passes through the nose into the stomach.","Colectomy is surgical removal of part or all of the colon, and appendectomy is removal of the appendix."],"s":[0,2.45,6.53,11.43,17.96,23.67,33.06,38.37,42.86],"f":[0,2.35,6.28,10.99,17.26,22.76,31.78,36.88,41.2]},{"t":["Here are the main G I drug classes.","Antacids neutralize stomach acid.","Proton pump inhibitors inhibit the gastric H plus K plus A T P ase, whereas H two receptor blockers reduce histamine-stimulated acid secretion.","Antiemetics prevent nausea and vomiting: anti, against, and emesis, vomiting.","Antidiarrheals reduce the frequency of stools, and laxatives promote bowel movements.","Antispasmodics relieve spasm of the G I muscle.","Digestive enzymes replace the natural enzymes.","Hepatobiliary treatments depend on the condition; there is no single drug class that generically supports every liver disorder.","And anti-inflammatory drugs reduce bowel inflammation in inflammatory bowel disease."],"s":[0,3.67,5.5,16.03,20.62,25.66,29.32,32.07,40.32],"f":[0,3.54,5.32,15.5,19.93,24.81,28.35,31.01,38.98]},{"t":["Now we move to the urinary system.","Remember this sentence: the kidneys make urine, the ureters carry it, the bladder stores it, and the urethra carries it out of the body.","The two kidneys sit high at the back of the abdomen, the ureters run down to the urinary bladder in the pelvis, and the urethra carries urine out of the body.","The blood vessels bring blood to the kidneys to be filtered.","The main functions are renal filtration, regulation of fluid, electrolytes and acid-base balance, and excretion of metabolic waste; the urinary bladder stores urine."],"s":[0,2.38,10.52,21.04,24.78],"f":[0,2.28,10.1,20.2,23.78]},{"t":["Here are the urinary roots.","Nephro and reno both mean kidney, as in nephrology and renal.","Uro means urine or the urinary tract, as in urology, and urino means urine, as in urinalysis.","Cysto means the bladder, as in cystitis, and vesico also means the urinary bladder, as in vesicoureteral reflux.","Uretero is the ureter, as in ureterolithiasis, and urethro is the urethra, as in urethritis.","Be careful, these two look very similar.","Pyelo is the renal pelvis, as in pyelonephritis.","Litho means stone, or calculus, as in lithotripsy.","Glomerulo is the glomerulus, as in glomerulonephritis.","And albumino is albumin, a protein, as in albuminuria."],"s":[0,2.19,6.48,13.88,21.78,28.96,31.55,35.36,39.07,43.41],"f":[0,1.83,5.74,12.54,19.74,26.43,28.87,32.35,35.74,39.57]},{"t":["If we cut a kidney open, we see three regions.","The cortex is the outer layer, where filtration begins.","The medulla is the inner part, made of pyramids, where urine is concentrated.","And the pelvis is the funnel that carries urine to the ureter.","Between the pyramids are the renal columns.","Urine drips from the tip of each pyramid, the renal papilla, into a minor calyx, then into a major calyx, and then into the renal pelvis."],"s":[0,3.25,6.95,11.69,15.58,18.52],"f":[0,2.85,6.09,10.55,14.16,16.95]},{"t":["The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney, and each kidney has about one million of them.","Blood enters through the afferent arteriole into the glomerulus, a ball of capillaries, and leaves through the efferent arteriole.","The glomerulus sits inside Bowman's capsule, and this is where filtration begins.","The filtrate then flows through the proximal convoluted tubule, where most reabsorption happens, then down and up the loop of Henle, then through the distal convoluted tubule, and finally into the collecting duct.","The peritubular capillaries and the vasa recta surround the tubules to take back what the body needs."],"s":[0,6.67,14.43,19.95,32.69],"f":[0,6.23,13.07,18.09,29.6]},{"t":["This slide shows the tubes that carry, store and remove urine.","The ureter is a muscular tube that carries urine from the kidney to the bladder by peristalsis.","The bladder is a muscular bag that stores urine.","Its wall contains the detrusor muscle, which contracts to empty the bladder.","The urethra carries urine out of the body.","Notice the difference between the sexes: the male urethra is long and passes through the prostate and the penis, while the female urethra is short, which is one reason why urinary infections are more common in women."],"s":[0,3.86,10.08,13.36,18.01,20.91],"f":[0,3.45,9.26,12.19,16.5,19.15]},{"t":["These terms describe changes in urine and urination.","Remember that uria means urine.","Oliguria is low urine output, and polyuria is high urine output.","Anuria is extremely low or essentially absent urine output, often defined clinically as less than one hundred millilitres in twenty-four hours in an adult.","Hematuria is blood in the urine, and proteinuria is protein in the urine.","Dysuria is painful urination.","Urgency is a sudden strong urge to urinate.","Frequency means urinating more often.","And nocturia means urinating at night: noct means night."],"s":[0,3.39,5.51,10.17,20.33,25.84,27.53,30.92,33.04],"f":[0,3.21,5.22,9.64,19.28,24.5,26.1,29.32,31.33]},{"t":["Here are the common diseases of the urinary system.","A urinary tract infection, or U T I, is a bacterial infection of the urinary tract.","Urinary stones are hard mineral deposits that may form in the kidney and lodge elsewhere in the urinary tract.","Glomerulonephritis is inflammation of the glomeruli.","Chronic kidney disease, C K D, is a gradual loss of kidney function over months or years, while acute kidney injury, A K I, is a sudden decline in kidney function.","And benign prostatic hyperplasia is non-cancerous prostate enlargement that can cause bladder outlet obstruction, especially in older men."],"s":[0,3.58,9.93,17.48,19.87,32.19],"f":[0,3.38,9.39,16.52,18.78,30.42]},{"t":["How do we examine the kidneys and urinary tract?","Urinalysis checks the composition of urine for abnormal findings.","A urine culture can identify and quantify organisms growing in urine and, when indicated, support antimicrobial susceptibility testing; results require clinical interpretation.","Serum creatinine and urea nitrogen are measured in blood, while e G F R is estimated from creatinine, sometimes with cystatin C, together with factors such as age and sex.","Ultrasound shows kidney size, cysts and the bladder.","A C T scan detects stones and obstruction.","And cystoscopy views the bladder and urethra."],"s":[0,3.88,7.75,17.23,30.15,33.59,37.04],"f":[0,3.71,7.41,16.47,28.82,32.11,35.41]},{"t":["These are the urinary procedures.","Catheterization uses a tube to drain urine from the bladder.","Cystoscopy uses a scope to view the urethra and bladder.","Ureteroscopy passes a scope into the ureter, often to remove stones.","Lithotripsy uses shock waves to break kidney stones: litho, stone, and tripsy, crushing.","Dialysis uses a machine to filter the blood when the kidneys fail.","And a kidney biopsy uses a needle to remove a small piece of kidney tissue."],"s":[0,2.7,6.99,10.95,15.86,21.74,26.2],"f":[0,2.28,6.22,9.85,14.31,19.84,23.89]},{"t":["Finally, the urinary drug classes.","Antibiotics treat urinary tract infections.","Diuretics increase urine output.","Alpha blockers relax the muscle and improve urine flow in benign prostatic hyperplasia.","Antimuscarinics reduce bladder spasm and urgency.","Five-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the prostate.","And urinary alkalinizers raise the p H of the urine.","That completes the content of this lecture."],"s":[0,2.65,5.89,8.33,14.44,18.17,21.45,25.45],"f":[0,2.56,5.62,7.94,13.47,16.92,20.12,23.88]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 1 to 5.","Question 1. The term dysphagia means: A, Painful digestion. B, Vomiting blood. C, Watery stool. D, Difficulty swallowing. The answer is D, Difficulty swallowing.","Question 2. The suffix -itis means: A, Enlargement. B, Inflammation. C, Stone formation. D, Discharge. The answer is B, Inflammation.","Question 3. The combining form gastr o means: A, Stomach. B, Liver. C, Colon. D, Bile duct. The answer is A, Stomach.","Question 4. The term hematemesis means: A, Black stool. B, Blood in urine. C, Vomiting blood. D, Yellow skin. The answer is C, Vomiting blood.","Question 5. The combining form hepat o means: A, Pancreas. B, Liver. C, Intestine. D, Esophagus. The answer is B, Liver."],"s":[0,2.6,18.78,33.97,47.98,63.37],"f":[0,2.53,17.31,31.3,44.39,58.39]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. The suffix -megaly means: A, Pain. B, Surgical repair. C, Narrowing. D, Enlargement. The answer is D, Enlargement.","Question 7. The term cholelithiasis means: A, Inflamed colon. B, Vomiting. C, Gallstone formation. D, Stomach pain. The answer is C, Gallstone formation.","Question 8. The combining form enter o means: A, Intestine. B, Mouth. C, Rectum. D, Stomach. The answer is A, Intestine.","Question 9. The term melena means: A, Bright red stool. B, Loose stool. C, Constipation. D, Black tarry stool. The answer is D, Black tarry stool.","Question 10. The suffix -rrhea means: A, Vomiting. B, Flow or discharge. C, Stone. D, Tumor. The answer is B, Flow or discharge."],"s":[0,2.6,17.02,33.15,47.43,62.76],"f":[0,2.45,15.95,30.86,43.95,57.91]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 11 to 15.","Question 11. Which organ begins protein digestion? A, Stomach. B, Esophagus. C, Large intestine. D, Gallbladder. The answer is A, Stomach.","Question 12. Primary site of nutrient absorption: A, Esophagus. B, Rectum. C, Small intestine. D, Gallbladder. The answer is C, Small intestine.","Question 13. The gallbladder mainly functions to: A, Produce insulin. B, Store and concentrate bile. C, Absorb water. D, Produce saliva. The answer is B, Store and concentrate bile.","Question 14. Peristalsis refers to: A, Chemical digestion. B, Blood filtration. C, Bile production. D, Wave-like propulsion. The answer is D, Wave-like propulsion.","Question 15. Villi mainly increase: A, Surface area for absorption. B, Gastric acid. C, Fecal storage. D, Pancreatic secretion. The answer is A, Surface area for absorption."],"s":[0,3.14,19.13,34.67,52.25,68.73],"f":[0,2.68,17.35,31.89,47.98,63.4]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 16 to 20.","Question 16. GERD is reflux of acid into the: A, Colon. B, Esophagus. C, Pancreas. D, Appendix. The answer is B, Esophagus.","Question 17. Colonoscopy mainly examines the: A, Liver and bile ducts. B, Stomach only. C, Colon and rectum. D, Oral cavity. The answer is C, Colon and rectum.","Question 18. Test for liver enzymes and bilirubin: A, CK. B, Troponin. C, Urinalysis. D, LFTs. The answer is D, LFTs.","Question 19. A proton pump inhibitor reduces: A, Gastric acid secretion. B, Intestinal motility. C, Saliva production. D, Bile storage. The answer is A, Gastric acid secretion.","Question 20. Appendicitis is inflammation of the: A, Gallbladder. B, Appendix. C, Pancreas. D, Rectum. The answer is B, Appendix."],"s":[0,2.99,18.02,34.5,50.44,68.7],"f":[0,2.71,16.54,32.05,46.78,63.48]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 21 to 25.","Question 21. The combining form nephr o means: A, Bladder. B, Ureter. C, Kidney. D, Urine. The answer is C, Kidney.","Question 22. The combining form cyst o refers to the: A, Urethra. B, Bladder. C, Kidney. D, Renal pelvis. The answer is B, Bladder.","Question 23. The suffix -uria relates to: A, Blood pressure. B, Kidney capsule. C, Urine or urination. D, Stone formation. The answer is C, Urine or urination.","Question 24. Hematuria means: A, Glucose in urine. B, Pus in urine. C, Blood in urine. D, Protein in urine. The answer is C, Blood in urine.","Question 25. Oliguria means: A, Excess urine output. B, Painful urination. C, Night urination. D, Decreased urine output. The answer is D, Decreased urine output."],"s":[0,3.23,17.24,32.16,49.15,65.12],"f":[0,3.05,16.06,30.12,45.81,60.17]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 26 to 30.","Question 26. Polyuria means: A, Decreased urine output. B, Excess urine output. C, Blood in urine. D, Retained urine. The answer is B, Excess urine output.","Question 27. Dysuria means: A, Painful urination. B, No urine output. C, Urination at night. D, Protein in urine. The answer is A, Painful urination.","Question 28. Nocturia means: A, Daytime frequency only. B, Bladder infection. C, Kidney stone. D, Urination at night. The answer is D, Urination at night.","Question 29. Proteinuria means: A, Protein in urine. B, Bacteria in urine. C, Blood in urine. D, Urine retention. The answer is A, Protein in urine.","Question 30. Anuria means: A, Urine infection. B, Complete bladder emptying. C, Absence of urine output. D, Pain in the kidney. The answer is C, Absence of urine output."],"s":[0,2.98,19.31,35.79,52.31,68.43],"f":[0,2.73,18.1,33.5,48.84,63.66]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 31 to 35.","Question 31. The functional unit of the kidney is the: A, Ureter. B, Renal pelvis. C, Bladder. D, Nephron. The answer is D, Nephron.","Question 32. Filtration begins in the: A, Loop of Henle. B, Glomerulus. C, Ureter. D, Urethra. The answer is B, Glomerulus.","Question 33. Urine travels from kidney to bladder through the: A, Ureter. B, Urethra. C, Renal artery. D, Nephron. The answer is A, Ureter.","Question 34. Main function of the urinary bladder: A, Filter blood. B, Produce hormones. C, Store urine. D, Make red blood cells. The answer is C, Store urine.","Question 35. The tube carrying urine out of the body: A, Ureter. B, Renal vein. C, Cortex. D, Urethra. The answer is D, Urethra."],"s":[0,3.25,18.17,33.04,48.53,65.07],"f":[0,3,16.92,30.78,45.39,60.39]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 36 to 40.","Question 36. Most reabsorption occurs in the: A, Proximal tubule. B, Renal pelvis. C, Ureter. D, Bowman's space. The answer is A, Proximal tubule.","Question 37. The renal cortex mainly contains: A, Major calyces. B, Renal corpuscles. C, Ureteric muscle. D, Detrusor muscle. The answer is B, Renal corpuscles.","Question 38. The detrusor muscle is found in the: A, Kidney capsule. B, Ureter wall. C, Bladder wall. D, Renal pelvis. The answer is C, Bladder wall.","Question 39. Kidney stones commonly cause: A, Flank pain, hematuria. B, Night blindness. C, Hearing loss. D, Hyperglycemia. The answer is A, Flank pain, hematuria.","Question 40. Imaging test for kidney size or hydronephrosis: A, Electrocardiogram. B, Spirometry. C, EEG. D, Ultrasound. The answer is D, Ultrasound."],"s":[0,3.01,18.9,36.28,51.93,68.95],"f":[0,2.72,17.47,33.64,48.11,63.95]}],"6":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture six.","Today we cover two systems that are closely linked by hormones: the reproductive system, which allows us to have children, and the endocrine system, the glands that send chemical messages through the blood.","As a chemical pathologist, hormones are a big part of my daily work, so I hope you will enjoy this lecture."],"s":[0,1.89,14.26],"f":[0,1.8,13.05]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","First, use the reproductive and endocrine word roots: female, male and glandular.","Second, name the main reproductive organs and endocrine glands.","Third, recognise the common signs and disorders of these two systems.","And fourth, know the main tests, procedures and drug classes.","At the end, you will test yourself with forty multiple-choice questions."],"s":[0,3.71,9.25,13.79,18.76,22.79],"f":[0,3.36,8.22,12.34,16.47,20.12]},{"t":["The male and female systems work together for reproduction.","Compare them on this slide.","The gametes, or sex cells, are the sperm in the male and the ovum, or egg, in the female.","The main hormones are testosterone in the male, and estrogen and progesterone in the female.","And the main organs are the testes, the prostate and the penis in the male, and the ovaries, the uterus and the vagina in the female."],"s":[0,3.56,5.39,10.81,16.65],"f":[0,3.47,5.08,10.16,15.68]},{"t":["Here are the female reproductive roots.","Gyneco means woman or female, as in gynecology.","Oophoro and ovaro both mean ovary, as in oophorectomy and ovarian cyst.","Salpingo means the fallopian tube, as in salpingectomy.","Three roots mean the uterus: hystero, as in hysterectomy, metro, as in metroplasty, and utero, as in uterine fibroid.","Cervico is the cervix, as in cervicitis.","Vagino and colpo both mean the vagina, as in vaginitis and colposcopy.","Vulvo is the vulva.","And mammo and masto both refer to the breast, as in mammogram and mastectomy."],"s":[0,2.58,6.01,11.16,14.6,22.75,25.76,30.91,32.62],"f":[0,2.47,5.77,10.72,14.02,21.85,24.73,29.68,31.33]},{"t":["Now the male reproductive roots.","Andro means male, as in andrology.","Orcho, orchio and testo all mean the testicle, as in orchitis and testicular.","Prostato is the prostate gland, as in prostatitis.","Epididymo is the epididymis, as in epididymitis.","Vaso means a vessel or duct, as in vasectomy, cutting the vas deferens.","Spermato and spermo mean sperm, as in spermatogenesis, the making of sperm, and spermatozoon, a single mature sperm cell.","And balano is the glans penis, as in balanitis."],"s":[0,2.37,5.2,10.57,14.48,18.29,23.39,31.65],"f":[0,2.12,4.6,9.6,13.11,16.53,21.01,28.6]},{"t":["This slide shows the male reproductive system.","The testes, inside the scrotum, produce sperm and testosterone.","Sperm mature and are stored in the epididymis, the coiled tube on the back of each testis.","They then travel up the vas deferens, which joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct.","This passes through the prostate gland, which sits just below the urinary bladder, and opens into the urethra, which runs through the penis.","Notice that in the male, the urethra carries both urine and semen."],"s":[0,3.11,7.44,13.05,20.36,29.1],"f":[0,2.78,6.95,11.96,18.53,26.35]},{"t":["This slide shows the female reproductive system.","The two ovaries produce the ova and the female hormones.","The uterine, or fallopian, tubes carry the ovum toward the uterus, and their finger-like ends, the fimbriae, catch the ovum when it is released.","The uterus has a fundus at the top, a body, and a cervix at the bottom, which opens into the vagina.","The wall of the uterus has three layers: the endometrium, the inner lining; the myometrium, the muscular layer; and the perimetrium, the outer covering.","In the ovary, follicles develop from primordial, to primary, to secondary, to the mature Graafian follicle, which releases the ovum and then becomes the corpus luteum."],"s":[0,3.28,7.01,15.96,22.68,31.71],"f":[0,3.01,6.38,14.62,20.54,28.92]},{"t":["This slide shows the external genitalia.","In the male, the penis contains three columns of erectile tissue: two corpora cavernosa and one corpus spongiosum, which surrounds the urethra.","The male urethra has several parts: the prostatic urethra, the membranous urethra, and the spongy, or penile, urethra, which ends at the external urethral orifice.","In the female, the external genitalia are called the vulva.","They include the labia majora and labia minora, the clitoris, the urethral orifice, and the vaginal orifice."],"s":[0,2.94,12.56,23.5,27.27],"f":[0,2.69,11.37,21.41,24.91]},{"t":["Sperm are made in the seminiferous tubules of the testis, in a process called spermatogenesis.","The cells develop in order: spermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, and finally the spermatozoa.","The Sertoli cells support and nourish them.","A mature sperm has three parts.","The head contains the nucleus, with twenty-three chromosomes, and is capped by the acrosome, which holds enzymes to penetrate the ovum.","The midpiece is packed with mitochondria, which produce the energy for movement.","And the tail, or flagellum, moves the sperm forward."],"s":[0,6.21,14.84,17.58,19.91,28.29,33.05],"f":[0,5.75,13.59,16.1,18.15,25.85,30.07]},{"t":["The menstrual cycle commonly lasts about twenty-eight days, but normal adult cycles vary, often around twenty-four to thirty-eight days, and ovarian and uterine phases occur in parallel.","In the uterus, the menstrual phase, days one to five, is when the lining sheds and bleeding occurs.","In the proliferative phase, days six to thirteen, the endometrium repairs and thickens under estrogen.","In a twenty-eight-day example, ovulation occurs around day fourteen, but its timing varies with cycle length.","And in the secretory phase, days fifteen to twenty-eight, progesterone prepares the lining for implantation.","In the ovary, the follicular phase, days one to thirteen, grows the follicle.","At ovulation the mature follicle ruptures and releases the ovum.","Then in the luteal phase, the corpus luteum forms and makes progesterone.","If there is no pregnancy, it regresses, and the cycle starts again."],"s":[0,11.01,18.34,24.46,31.39,37.5,42.8,46.88,51.77],"f":[0,10.59,17.65,23.53,30.2,36.08,41.18,45.1,49.81]},{"t":["These words describe common reproductive problems.","Clinically, infertility generally refers to failure to achieve pregnancy after twelve months of regular unprotected intercourse, with earlier evaluation appropriate in some circumstances.","Amenorrhea means no menstrual periods, dysmenorrhea means painful menstruation, and menorrhagia means heavy menstrual bleeding.","Notice the root meno, meaning month or menstruation.","Leukorrhea is a white vaginal discharge.","Dyspareunia is painful intercourse.","Galactorrhea is milk-like nipple discharge unrelated to normal pregnancy or breastfeeding.","Erectile dysfunction is persistent difficulty achieving or maintaining an erection sufficient for satisfactory sexual activity.","Oligospermia is a low sperm count, azoospermia is no sperm in the semen, and hematospermia is blood in the semen.","And gynecomastia is an enlarged male breast."],"s":[0,2.85,13.76,20.87,24.67,27.51,29.41,34.63,41.75,51.23],"f":[0,2.76,13.33,20.22,23.9,26.66,28.5,33.55,40.45,49.64]},{"t":["Here are common disorders in men.","Benign prostatic hyperplasia is an enlarged prostate that causes urinary symptoms.","Prostatitis is an inflamed prostate.","Erectile dysfunction is often caused by reduced blood flow to the penis.","Testicular torsion is twisting of the spermatic cord, and it is a surgical emergency, because the testis can lose its blood supply.","Epididymitis is an inflamed epididymis.","And a hydrocele is fluid around the testis, while a varicocele is dilated veins in the scrotum."],"s":[0,2.39,8.34,11.23,16.12,24.6,27.83],"f":[0,2.19,7.5,10.32,14.92,22.78,25.64]},{"t":["And common disorders in women.","Polycystic ovary syndrome, or P C O S, is a heterogeneous endocrine-metabolic disorder. Diagnosis may involve ovulatory dysfunction, hyperandrogenism and polycystic ovarian morphology; ovarian cysts are not required.","An ovarian cyst is a fluid-filled sac on an ovary.","Endometriosis is the presence of endometrium-like tissue outside the uterus.","Uterine fibroids are benign growths in the muscular wall of the uterus.","Pelvic inflammatory disease is infection and inflammation of the upper female genital tract, including the uterus, tubes or nearby tissues.","Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor of the cervix, strongly associated with persistent high-risk H P V infection; screening detects precancerous changes and some early cancers."],"s":[0,2.24,14.78,19.26,23.74,29.11,38.07],"f":[0,2.19,14.43,18.81,23.18,28.43,37.18]},{"t":["How do we examine the reproductive system?","A pelvic ultrasound images the pelvic organs, and transvaginal or transrectal ultrasound gives a close-up internal view.","Semen analysis checks the sperm count and quality.","Hormone testing may include F S H, L H, estradiol, progesterone, testosterone and prolactin, selected according to the clinical question and cycle phase.","Hysteroscopy views the uterine cavity, while colposcopy examines the cervix and often the vagina or vulva with magnification.","And M R I or C T of the pelvis gives detailed images."],"s":[0,3,10.27,13.7,23.54,31.25],"f":[0,2.84,9.73,12.97,22.29,29.58]},{"t":["Here are the reproductive procedures.","In the male: vasectomy cuts or seals the vas deferens; prostatectomy removes the prostate; orchiectomy removes a testicle; and circumcision removes the foreskin.","Laparoscopy is a minimally invasive surgical or diagnostic procedure using an endoscope inserted through small abdominal incisions.","In the female: hysterectomy removes the uterus; oophorectomy removes an ovary; salpingectomy removes a uterine or fallopian tube; and dilation and curettage, D and C, dilates the cervix and scrapes the uterine lining.","Notice how many of these words end in ectomy, surgical removal."],"s":[0,2.3,12.89,20.72,35.92],"f":[0,2.13,11.9,19.13,33.16]},{"t":["These are the main drug classes for the reproductive system.","Hormonal contraceptives prevent pregnancy through various mechanisms, including suppression of ovulation and changes to cervical mucus or the endometrium, depending on the formulation.","Estrogen therapy replaces estrogen, progesterone therapy supports the uterine lining, and testosterone therapy restores testosterone.","Fertility drugs stimulate ovulation or sperm production.","Continuous G n R H agonist treatment eventually suppresses L H and F S H after an initial flare; G n R H antagonists inhibit their release directly.","Antiandrogens block the effects of male hormones.","And antibiotics and antifungals treat genital infections."],"s":[0,4.44,14.67,21.33,24.44,36.89,40],"f":[0,4.27,14.08,20.48,23.47,35.42,38.41]},{"t":["Now we move to the endocrine system.","Endocrine glands release hormones directly into the blood.","The hypothalamus is the control centre, and the pituitary is the master gland.","The pineal gland controls sleep rhythms.","The thyroid controls metabolism, and the parathyroids control calcium balance.","The thymus supports immunity.","The adrenal glands handle the stress response.","The pancreas controls blood sugar.","And the gonads, the ovaries and testes, make the female and male hormones."],"s":[0,2.78,6.64,12.02,14.76,20.09,22.24,25.36,27.73],"f":[0,2.49,6.1,10.91,13.42,18.36,20.24,22.98,25.14]},{"t":["Here are the endocrine roots.","Adeno means gland, as in adenoma, a tumor of a gland.","Endocrino means internal secretion, as in endocrinology.","Crino means to secrete, as in endocrine.","Hormono means hormone, as in hormonal.","Pituitaro is the pituitary gland, as in hypopituitarism.","Thyroido is the thyroid gland, and parathyroido the parathyroid glands, as in thyroidectomy and parathyroidectomy.","Adreno is the adrenal gland, as in adrenalectomy.","Pancreato is the pancreas, as in pancreatectomy.","Pinealo is the pineal gland, as in pinealectomy, and thymo is the thymus, as in thymectomy.","And gonado means the ovary or testis, as in gonadectomy."],"s":[0,2.19,6.25,10.71,13.77,16.69,21.28,29.25,32.95,36.76,43.14],"f":[0,1.96,5.55,9.58,12.39,15,19.24,26.51,29.95,33.29,39.21]},{"t":["The hypothalamus, at the base of the brain, controls the pituitary gland, which hangs below it on a stalk.","The pituitary has two parts.","The anterior pituitary, or adenohypophysis, is glandular tissue that makes its own hormones: growth hormone for bone and muscle; T S H for the thyroid; A C T H for the adrenal cortex; F S H and L H for the ovaries and testes; and prolactin for the mammary glands.","The posterior pituitary, or neurohypophysis, stores hormones made by the hypothalamus: A D H, which controls water balance in the kidney, and oxytocin, which acts on the uterus and on milk ejection."],"s":[0,6.88,9.09,27.31],"f":[0,6.22,8.2,25.22]},{"t":["The thyroid is a butterfly-shaped gland in the front of the neck, below the larynx.","It controls metabolism.","Look at how it is regulated.","The hypothalamus releases T R H, which tells the anterior pituitary to release T S H, which tells the thyroid to release its hormones, T three and T four.","These act on target tissues all over the body.","High thyroid hormone then switches this chain off, which is called negative feedback.","Behind the thyroid are four small parathyroid glands.","They make parathyroid hormone, P T H, which controls calcium balance."],"s":[0,5,7.14,9.46,21.02,24.18,29.46,33.16],"f":[0,4.51,6.49,8.42,18.94,21.72,26.6,30.02]},{"t":["The adrenal glands sit on top of the kidneys, and they make stress hormones.","Each gland has two parts.","The outer cortex makes steroid hormones, such as cortisol and aldosterone.","The inner medulla makes epinephrine and norepinephrine, the fight or flight hormones.","The pancreas has small groups of hormone cells called the islets.","The beta cells make insulin, which lowers blood glucose, and the alpha cells make glucagon, which raises blood glucose."],"s":[0,4.48,6.49,11.63,16.7,20.85],"f":[0,4.11,5.93,10.51,15.19,18.9]},{"t":["These are the endocrine signs, related to the thyroid and to glucose.","A goiter is an enlarged thyroid.","Heat intolerance and cold intolerance mean being sensitive to heat or to cold; the first is typical of an overactive thyroid and the second of an underactive one.","A tremor is involuntary shaking, and tachycardia is a fast heart rate.","Then the three polys of diabetes: polyuria, excessive urination; polydipsia, excessive thirst; and polyphagia, excessive hunger.","Hypoglycemia is low blood glucose, and hyperglycemia is high blood glucose."],"s":[0,4.74,7.26,18.15,23.01,31.65],"f":[0,4.21,6.58,16.35,20.9,28.85]},{"t":["More endocrine terms, general, pituitary and adrenal.","Fatigue is extreme tiredness.","Weight gain and weight loss are common in thyroid disease.","Hirsutism is excessive terminal hair growth in a male-pattern distribution in females.","Gynecomastia is an enlarged male breast.","Short stature has many possible causes, not all related to growth hormone deficiency. Gigantism refers to excessive growth due to growth hormone excess before epiphyseal closure.","Acromegaly is enlargement of the hands, feet and face from too much growth hormone in adults.","Cushingoid features, such as a moon face and central obesity, come from too much cortisol.","And Addisonian pigmentation is darkened skin seen in adrenal failure."],"s":[0,2.99,4.71,8.98,14.12,16.69,27.81,34.65,41.07],"f":[0,2.83,4.45,8.49,13.35,15.77,26.29,32.76,38.82]},{"t":["Thyroid and parathyroid disorders are about too much or too little hormone.","A goiter is an enlarged thyroid gland.","Hyperthyroidism is increased synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormones by the gland; thyrotoxicosis is the clinical state of thyroid hormone excess from any cause. Hypothyroidism means insufficient thyroid hormone action.","Hyperparathyroidism is excess P T H, which causes high blood calcium.","And hypoparathyroidism is low P T H, which causes low blood calcium.","Notice how hyper and hypo do all the work in these terms."],"s":[0,5.07,8.02,20.69,25.34,30.41],"f":[0,4.91,7.77,20.03,24.53,29.43]},{"t":["Now diabetes mellitus and other pancreatic disorders.","Type one diabetes usually involves autoimmune loss of pancreatic beta cells, leading to severe insulin deficiency; residual secretion may remain early in disease.","Type two diabetes results from insulin resistance together with progressive beta-cell dysfunction and is the most common form.","An insulinoma is a tumor that makes excess insulin, causing hypoglycemia.","A glucagonoma makes excess glucagon, causing hyperglycemia and a skin rash.","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors may be functional and secrete hormones, or nonfunctional without a clinically important hormone syndrome.","Remember, oma means tumor."],"s":[0,3.21,13.78,22.04,27.09,32.14,39.95],"f":[0,3.08,13.2,21.12,25.95,30.79,38.27]},{"t":["How do we examine the endocrine glands?","Blood hormone tests measure hormone levels directly.","Glucose and H b A one c measure blood sugar; H b A one c reflects the average blood sugar over the last two to three months.","The thyroid function tests are T S H, T four and T three.","Cortisol and A C T H tests check adrenal function.","Thyroid ultrasound shows the thyroid structure.","C T and M R I find pituitary or adrenal tumors.","And a nuclear thyroid scan shows the active thyroid tissue."],"s":[0,2.89,6.39,15.2,19.52,23.54,26.86,31.39],"f":[0,2.5,5.81,13.66,17.61,21.34,24.46,28.45]},{"t":["These are the endocrine procedures.","Fine-needle aspiration takes cells from a thyroid nodule.","Thyroidectomy removes the thyroid, parathyroidectomy removes one or more parathyroid glands, and adrenalectomy removes an adrenal gland.","Transsphenoidal surgery removes a pituitary tumor through the nose: trans means through, and sphenoid is the bone behind the nose.","And an insulin pump delivers insulin, while a C G M, a continuous glucose monitor, tracks glucose all day."],"s":[0,2.66,6.66,15.99,24.35],"f":[0,2.33,6.04,14.75,22.35]},{"t":["Finally, the endocrine drug classes.","Insulin treats diabetes mellitus, and oral antidiabetic agents control type two diabetes.","Thyroid hormone replacement treats hypothyroidism, and antithyroid drugs treat hyperthyroidism.","Corticosteroids reduce inflammation, and mineralocorticoids treat adrenal insufficiency.","Dopamine agonists treat hyperprolactinemia, too much prolactin.","Somatostatin analogs treat acromegaly and neuroendocrine tumors.","And bisphosphonates, with calcium and vitamin D, treat osteoporosis.","That completes the content of this lecture."],"s":[0,2.8,9.64,16.65,22.91,27.52,32.64,37.6],"f":[0,2.69,9.11,15.61,21.4,25.64,30.25,34.82]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 1 to 5.","Question 1. andr o means: A, Male. B, Ovary. C, Uterus. D, Breast. The answer is A, Male.","Question 2. orch o means: A, Cervix. B, Vagina. C, A testis. D, Sperm. The answer is C, A testis.","Question 3. gynec o means: A, Prostate. B, A woman / female. C, Testis. D, Duct. The answer is B, A woman / female.","Question 4. oophor o means: A, Uterus. B, Cervix. C, Breast. D, Ovary. The answer is D, Ovary.","Question 5. salping o means: A, Uterine tube. B, Vagina. C, Prostate. D, Semen. The answer is A, Uterine tube."],"s":[0,2.6,15.14,28.59,43.15,56.13],"f":[0,2.53,14.16,26.52,39.88,51.88]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. hyster o means: A, Sperm. B, Vulva. C, Gland. D, Uterus. The answer is D, Uterus.","Question 7. cervic o means: A, Ovary. B, Cervix. C, Testis. D, Sperm. The answer is B, Cervix.","Question 8. vagin o or colp o means: A, Kidney. B, Bladder. C, Vagina. D, Prostate. The answer is C, Vagina.","Question 9. mast o or mamm o means: A, Breast. B, Uterus. C, Testis. D, Cervix. The answer is A, Breast.","Question 10. -ectomy means: A, Inflammation. B, Pain. C, Surgical removal. D, Discharge. The answer is C, Surgical removal."],"s":[0,2.61,15.64,28.71,42.83,56.15],"f":[0,2.45,14.59,26.84,39.83,52.37]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 11 to 15.","Question 11. Sperm are produced mainly in the: A, Prostate. B, Seminiferous tubules. C, Vas deferens. D, Urethra. The answer is B, Seminiferous tubules.","Question 12. Testosterone is produced mainly by: A, Sertoli cells. B, Endometrium. C, Cervix. D, Leydig cells. The answer is D, Leydig cells.","Question 13. The epididymis mainly functions in: A, Sperm maturation, storage. B, Ovum transport. C, Menstruation. D, Estrogen secretion. The answer is A, Sperm maturation, storage.","Question 14. The vas deferens carries sperm toward the: A, Ovary. B, Ejaculatory duct. C, Uterus. D, Cervix. The answer is B, Ejaculatory duct.","Question 15. The ovary mainly produces: A, Urine. B, Bile. C, Insulin. D, Ova and hormones. The answer is D, Ova and hormones."],"s":[0,3.14,19.14,34.82,53.02,69.27],"f":[0,2.67,17.62,32.16,49.11,64]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 16 to 20.","Question 16. Fertilization most commonly occurs in the: A, Cervix. B, Vagina. C, Ampulla of uterine tube. D, Endometrium. The answer is C, Ampulla of uterine tube.","Question 17. The endometrium is the: A, Inner uterine lining. B, Ovarian cortex. C, Prostate capsule. D, Scrotal wall. The answer is A, Inner uterine lining.","Question 18. Menorrhagia means: A, Absent menstruation. B, Painful intercourse. C, Low sperm count. D, Heavy menstrual bleeding. The answer is D, Heavy menstrual bleeding.","Question 19. Benign prostatic hyperplasia commonly causes: A, Ovulation. B, Urinary obstruction. C, Cervical discharge. D, Breast secretion. The answer is B, Urinary obstruction.","Question 20. Azoospermia means: A, Blood in semen. B, Painful menstruation. C, No sperm in semen. D, Ovarian cyst. The answer is C, No sperm in semen."],"s":[0,2.99,20.26,36.81,53.83,71.79],"f":[0,2.71,18.37,33.58,49.21,65.91]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 21 to 25.","Question 21. endocrin o means: A, Outer secretion. B, Internal secretion. C, Blood cell. D, Nervous tissue. The answer is B, Internal secretion.","Question 22. hormon o means: A, Stimulus. B, Enzyme. C, Receptor. D, Hormone. The answer is D, Hormone.","Question 23. thyroid o means: A, Thyroid gland. B, Parathyroid gland. C, Thymus gland. D, Salivary gland. The answer is A, Thyroid gland.","Question 24. adren o means: A, Kidney. B, Pancreas. C, Adrenal gland. D, Adrenal hormone. The answer is C, Adrenal gland.","Question 25. pituitar o means: A, Pineal gland. B, Parathyroid gland. C, Pituitary gland. D, Salivary gland. The answer is C, Pituitary gland."],"s":[0,3.23,19.15,32.8,48.55,63.34],"f":[0,3.05,17.48,29.97,44.39,57.97]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 26 to 30.","Question 26. pancreat o means: A, Stomach. B, Pancreas. C, Liver. D, Spleen. The answer is B, Pancreas.","Question 27. gonad o means: A, Ovary only. B, Testis only. C, Ovary or testis. D, Uterus. The answer is C, Ovary or testis.","Question 28. Hyperglycemia means: A, High blood glucose. B, Low blood glucose. C, No blood glucose. D, Normal blood glucose. The answer is A, High blood glucose.","Question 29. Hypoglycemia means: A, High blood glucose. B, No blood glucose. C, Normal blood glucose. D, Low blood glucose. The answer is D, Low blood glucose.","Question 30. Thyroidectomy means: A, Repair of thyroid. B, Removal of thyroid. C, Thyroid biopsy. D, Thyroid irradiation. The answer is B, Removal of thyroid."],"s":[0,2.98,16.6,31.96,48.8,65.77],"f":[0,2.73,15.36,29.62,45.16,60.78]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 31 to 35.","Question 31. Which gland is the master gland? A, Thyroid gland. B, Adrenal gland. C, Pituitary gland. D, Pancreas. The answer is C, Pituitary gland.","Question 32. Which hormone lowers blood glucose? A, Insulin. B, Glucagon. C, Cortisol. D, Epinephrine. The answer is A, Insulin.","Question 33. Which hormone raises blood glucose? A, Insulin. B, Oxytocin. C, Somatostatin. D, Glucagon. The answer is D, Glucagon.","Question 34. Which gland secretes T3 and T4? A, Parathyroid gland. B, Thyroid gland. C, Pituitary gland. D, Adrenal gland. The answer is B, Thyroid gland.","Question 35. Which hormone increases blood calcium? A, Parathyroid hormone. B, Calcitonin. C, Insulin. D, Aldosterone. The answer is A, Parathyroid hormone."],"s":[0,3.25,20.27,35.78,52.23,70.12],"f":[0,3.04,18.56,32.85,47.79,64.33]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 36 to 40.","Question 36. Which adrenal region secretes epinephrine? A, Zona glomerulosa. B, Zona fasciculata. C, Zona reticularis. D, Adrenal medulla. The answer is D, Adrenal medulla.","Question 37. Cortisol is mainly produced by the: A, Adrenal medulla. B, Pituitary gland. C, Adrenal cortex. D, Thyroid gland. The answer is C, Adrenal cortex.","Question 38. ADH conserves water by acting on the: A, Intestine. B, Kidney. C, Liver. D, Skin. The answer is B, Kidney.","Question 39. Melatonin is secreted by the: A, Pineal gland. B, Pituitary gland. C, Thyroid gland. D, Adrenal gland. The answer is A, Pineal gland.","Question 40. Diabetes mellitus is characterized by: A, Hypoglycemia. B, Hypocalcemia. C, Hypothyroidism. D, Hyperglycemia. The answer is D, Hyperglycemia."],"s":[0,3.01,21.92,39,53.54,69.91],"f":[0,2.72,20.03,35.9,49.59,64.55]}],"7":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture seven.","Today we cover three systems.","The nervous system, the body's control and communication network.","Mental health, the words we use to describe the mind and behaviour.","And the blood and lymphatic systems, which carry oxygen, fight infection and stop bleeding.","There are many new words today, but you will see the same building blocks we have used in every lecture."],"s":[0,1.91,4.31,8.41,12.76,18.34],"f":[0,1.86,3.97,7.63,11.45,16.57]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","First, use the nervous, mental health and blood roots, such as neuro, psycho, hemo and lympho.","Second, describe the main parts of the nervous system, the blood and the lymphatic system.","Third, recognise neurologic, mental health and hematologic signs and disorders.","And fourth, know the main tests, procedures and drug classes.","At the end, you will test yourself with forty multiple-choice questions."],"s":[0,3.71,9.64,14.85,20.5,24.53],"f":[0,3.36,9.14,13.9,19.2,22.85]},{"t":["The nervous system has two main divisions.","The central nervous system, the C N S, is the brain and the spinal cord.","The brain processes information and controls our higher functions, and the spinal cord carries impulses to and from the brain and controls reflexes.","The peripheral nervous system, the P N S, is all the nerves outside them.","The somatic part is voluntary, and it controls the skin, muscles and joints.","The autonomic part is involuntary.","Its sympathetic division is fight or flight, which mobilizes energy, and its parasympathetic division is rest and digest, which conserves energy."],"s":[0,2.87,7.68,17.38,22.08,27.17,29.68],"f":[0,2.65,7.08,16.2,20.25,24.98,27.46]},{"t":["Here are the nervous system roots.","Neuro means nerve, as in neuritis.","Encephalo means brain, as in encephalitis.","Cerebro is the cerebrum, as in cerebrovascular, and cerebello is the cerebellum, as in cerebellar.","Myelo can mean spinal cord or bone marrow depending on context; myelitis is inflammation of the spinal cord and myelopoiesis means marrow blood-cell formation.","Meningo is the meninges, the coverings of the brain, as in meningitis.","Glio is the neuroglia, the support cells, as in glioma.","Radiculo is the nerve root, as in radiculopathy.","Ganglio is a ganglion, a cluster of nerve cells.","And synapso is the synapse, the junction between two nerve cells."],"s":[0,2.59,5.18,7.77,14.24,24.59,29.77,34.09,37.54,41.42],"f":[0,2.43,4.86,7.29,13.36,23.08,27.93,31.98,35.22,38.86]},{"t":["These roots describe the mind and behaviour.","Psycho and phreno both mean the mind, as in psychology and schizophrenia.","In psychiatric terminology, thym-o refers to mood or emotion, as in dysthymia. In other contexts, thym-o refers to the thymus gland; always interpret the term in context.","Phobo means fear, as in phobia.","Manio means madness, as in mania.","Depresso means low mood, as in depression, and anxio means anxiety.","Somno means sleep, as in insomnia, the inability to sleep.","Mnemo means memory, as in amnesia, loss of memory.","And cogno means cognition, thinking and knowing."],"s":[0,3.16,8.57,20.75,23.45,26.16,31.12,35.63,39.69],"f":[0,2.99,8.11,19.63,22.2,24.76,29.45,33.72,37.56]},{"t":["This slide shows the brain.","It has four major regions: the cerebrum, the diencephalon, the brainstem and the cerebellum.","The cerebrum has right and left hemispheres, and each has four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital.","Different areas have different jobs.","The motor area controls movement, and the somatosensory area receives touch.","The visual area is in the occipital lobe, and the auditory area is in the temporal lobe.","Broca's area controls speech, and Wernicke's area understands language.","The limbic system handles emotion and memory, the thalamus is the relay station, and the hypothalamus controls homeostasis.","Finally, the brainstem has three parts, the midbrain, the pons and the medulla, and the cerebellum behind it coordinates movement and balance."],"s":[0,2.01,8.09,15.15,17.49,22.67,28.41,32.99,41.2],"f":[0,1.73,7.16,13.68,15.98,20.63,25.65,29.73,37.5]},{"t":["These are common signs of nervous system problems.","A headache is pain in the head.","Vertigo is an illusory sensation of self-motion or environmental motion, commonly spinning; imbalance without perceived motion is not necessarily vertigo.","A seizure is abnormal electrical activity in the brain.","A tremor is rhythmic shaking.","Paralysis is loss of movement, and paresis is weakness of movement.","Paresthesia is tingling or numbness.","Syncope is a brief loss of consciousness, a faint.","Ataxia is poor coordination.","Aphasia is impaired language: a means without, and phasia means speech.","And neuropathy is nerve dysfunction."],"s":[0,3.64,6.82,15.91,20,22.27,27.27,29.54,33.63,35.45,40.45],"f":[0,3.4,6.37,14.86,18.68,20.81,25.48,27.6,31.42,33.12,37.79]},{"t":["These are common diseases of the brain, spinal cord and nerves.","Stroke is an acute neurological injury caused by disrupted cerebral blood flow, either by arterial blockage, called ischemic stroke, or by bleeding, called hemorrhagic stroke.","Epilepsy is a brain disorder characterized by an enduring predisposition to generate unprovoked seizures; recurrent unprovoked seizures are a common presentation.","Parkinson disease causes tremor and rigidity.","Alzheimer disease causes a decline in memory and thinking.","Multiple sclerosis is demyelination, a loss of the protective myelin coat of nerves in the C N S.","Meningitis is inflamed meninges, and encephalitis is an inflamed brain.","Peripheral neuropathy is damage to the peripheral nerves.","Migraine is a severe, recurrent headache.","And cerebral palsy is a motor disorder caused by early brain injury."],"s":[0,4.68,15.33,24.27,26.82,30.66,38.32,42.58,45.98,48.54],"f":[0,4.51,14.77,23.38,25.84,29.53,36.92,41.02,44.3,46.76]},{"t":["Now the signs used in mental health.","Anxiety is excessive worry.","Depressed mood is persistent sadness.","Mood swings are shifts in mood.","Insomnia is trouble sleeping.","Fatigue is low energy.","Poor concentration is trouble focusing.","Agitation means being restless and irritable.","And anhedonia means no pleasure in activities that used to be enjoyable: an means without, and hedonia means pleasure."],"s":[0,2.57,4.89,7.47,9.77,12.06,14.07,17.02,20.42],"f":[0,2.16,4.26,6.61,8.58,10.68,12.59,15.32,18.47]},{"t":["These are three common groups of mood and anxiety disorders.","Anxiety disorders cause fear and worry, with tension, palpitations and sweating.","Depressive disorders cause low mood, low energy, poor sleep and loss of interest.","And bipolar disorder means the mood swings between two poles: depressive episodes at one end, and manic or hypomanic episodes, with high energy and elevated mood, at the other.","Bi means two, and polar means poles."],"s":[0,4.27,9.45,14.73,26.49],"f":[0,3.57,8.38,13.2,23.9]},{"t":["These are psychotic and other disorders.","Schizophrenia causes hallucinations and disorganized thinking.","Schizoaffective disorder is psychosis with mood episodes.","Brief psychotic disorder is a sudden, short psychosis, and delusional disorder involves fixed false beliefs.","Major neurocognitive disorder, often called dementia, involves acquired cognitive decline affecting independence; memory may be affected but is not always the main symptom. Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness.","Notice the difference: dementia is slow, delirium is sudden.","Neurodevelopmental disorders include A D H D, with inattention and hyperactivity, autism spectrum disorder, with differences in social communication, and intellectual disability.","Finally, the substance-related terms: substance use disorder is compulsive use despite harm, intoxication is recent use with impaired judgment, and withdrawal is the symptoms after stopping."],"s":[0,2.92,5.84,9.24,16.53,32.58,36.96,47.65],"f":[0,2.83,5.67,8.98,16.06,31.65,35.91,46.3]},{"t":["How do we examine the nervous system?","M R I gives detailed images of the brain and spinal cord, and C T gives cross-sectional brain images.","An E E G records the electrical activity of the brain.","A nerve conduction study measures the speed of nerve signals.","A P E T scan shows brain metabolism and activity.","A lumbar puncture collects cerebrospinal fluid from the lower back.","For procedures: a stereotactic biopsy is an image-guided brain biopsy; deep brain stimulation uses electrodes to control movement; a nerve block uses an anesthetic to block pain signals; and neurological rehabilitation improves function and skills."],"s":[0,2.39,9.9,13.99,17.93,22.15,26.37],"f":[0,2.12,8.82,12.6,16.1,19.86,23.89]},{"t":["Here are the main drug classes for neurologic and mental health disorders.","Anticonvulsants prevent or reduce seizures.","Antidepressants improve mood and motivation.","Antipsychotics treat psychosis.","Anxiolytics reduce anxiety: anxio, anxiety, and lytic, breaking down.","Cognitive enhancers improve thinking in dementia.","And migraine medications relieve or prevent migraine."],"s":[0,4.99,8.03,11.12,13.59,18.51,22.14],"f":[0,4.5,7.42,10.37,12.58,17.1,20.26]},{"t":["We now move to the blood and lymphatic systems.","Hemo and hemato mean blood, as in hematology.","Erythro means red, as in erythrocyte, and leuko means white, as in leukocyte.","Thrombo means clot or platelet, as in thrombosis.","Sidero and ferro mean iron, as in ferropenia, a lack of iron.","Anemia means hemoglobin concentration below the expected reference threshold for age, sex or physiological state; it does not simply mean lack of blood.","Lympho is lymph, as in lymphocyte.","Adeno means gland, as in lymphadenitis, an inflamed lymph node.","Nodo means node, angio means vessel, as in lymphangioma, spleno is the spleen, as in splenomegaly, and in this anatomical context thym-o refers to the thymus, as in thymectomy, rather than the psychiatric root relating to mood."],"s":[0,3.93,7.43,13.11,16.61,21.86,31.91,34.54,38.91],"f":[0,3.69,6.97,12.29,15.57,20.49,29.91,32.37,36.47]},{"t":["Blood has three major functions.","Transport: it carries oxygen, nutrients and hormones.","Regulation: it controls p H, temperature and fluid balance.","And protection: it defends against pathogens.","Blood is made of plasma, about fifty-five percent, and formed elements, about forty-five percent.","Plasma is water with proteins and dissolved substances.","Red blood cells make up about forty-four percent and carry oxygen.","White blood cells and platelets together make up less than one percent; white cells defend the body, and platelets form clots."],"s":[0,2.34,6.19,10.53,13.58,19.64,23.61,28.11],"f":[0,2.1,5.64,9.76,12.51,18.16,21.83,25.83]},{"t":["The formed elements are the cells and cell fragments of blood.","Erythrocytes, the red blood cells, carry oxygen and have no nucleus.","Leukocytes, the white blood cells, defend the body and do have a nucleus.","Platelets participate in primary hemostasis; hemostasis also includes vascular responses and the coagulation cascade, followed by fibrinolysis.","Look at the normal counts per microlitre: red cells about four point two to six million, white cells four thousand to eleven thousand, and platelets one hundred and fifty thousand to four hundred and fifty thousand.","There are five types of white cell.","Neutrophils eat bacteria by phagocytosis.","Lymphocytes are the B and T cells.","Monocytes become macrophages.","Eosinophils fight parasites and act in allergy.","And basophils release histamine."],"s":[0,4.5,9,14.33,21.28,36.02,38.88,40.93,43.8,45.02,47.89],"f":[0,4.31,8.62,13.71,20.37,34.48,37.22,39.18,41.92,43.09,45.84]},{"t":["Blood is grouped by the antigens on the surface of the red cells.","In the ABO system, group A has the A antigen, group B has the B antigen, group AB has both, and group O has neither A nor B antigens on the red cells, although other red-cell antigens remain present.","In the Rh system, Rh-positive red cells carry the D antigen and Rh-negative cells do not.","Before giving blood, we always do ABO and Rh typing, then cross-matching, to make the transfusion safe.","The slide also shows fibrinolysis, the breakdown of a clot: plasminogen is turned into plasmin, which dissolves the fibrin clot."],"s":[0,4.96,19.83,25.93,32.41],"f":[0,4.68,18.73,24.5,30.62]},{"t":["The lymphatic system is a network of vessels, nodes and organs.","It returns fluid from the tissues to the blood and supports immunity.","Lymph flows through lymphatic vessels and lymph nodes, such as the cervical nodes in the neck and the inguinal nodes in the groin, and drains through the thoracic duct back into the venous circulation.","The thymus is where T cells mature.","The spleen filters the blood and removes old red blood cells.","The tonsils and adenoids are a first line of defence in the throat.","And Peyer's patches in the small intestine watch for germs in the gut."],"s":[0,4.25,8.62,20.61,23.31,27.49,31.67],"f":[0,3.94,7.91,18.72,21.17,24.8,28.78]},{"t":["These signs may be seen in blood disorders.","Pallor is reduced visible skin or mucosal coloration and may be associated with anemia, vasoconstriction or reduced peripheral perfusion.","Jaundice is yellow skin from high bilirubin.","Cyanosis is a bluish colour from low oxygen.","Petechiae are tiny, pinpoint bleeding spots in the skin, and ecchymosis is a large bruise.","And fatigue is a very common symptom of anemia."],"s":[0,3.17,10.7,13.48,16.65,22.59],"f":[0,3.07,10.37,13.06,16.13,21.89]},{"t":["These are common blood disorders, of the red cells, white cells, platelets and clotting.","Anemia is hemoglobin below the appropriate reference limit, often reducing the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood.","Polycythemia is too many red blood cells: poly, many, cyt, cell, and hemia, blood.","Leukemia is a cancer of the blood-forming cells.","Thrombocytopenia is low platelets, which increases the risk of bleeding: penia means deficiency.","Hemophilia is an inherited deficiency of a clotting factor.","And thrombosis is an abnormal blood clot."],"s":[0,6.22,12.89,19.11,22.67,28.45,32.45],"f":[0,6.1,12.63,18.73,22.22,27.88,31.8]},{"t":["Anemia has different causes.","Iron deficiency anemia gives small red cells, because there is not enough iron to make hemoglobin.","Megaloblastic anemia gives large red cells, from a lack of vitamin B twelve or folate: megalo means large.","Hemolytic anemia is caused by early destruction of red cells.","Sickle cell disease is caused by an abnormal hemoglobin that makes the red cells sickle-shaped.","And thalassemia is caused by reduced production of the globin chains of hemoglobin."],"s":[0,2.25,8.74,16.57,20.89,27.22],"f":[0,2.08,7.86,14.9,18.87,24.62]},{"t":["Now the white cell and bleeding disorders.","Leukocytosis is a high white cell count, and leukopenia is a low white cell count.","Neutropenia is low neutrophils, and lymphocytosis is high lymphocytes.","In leukemia, abnormal white cells crowd the bone marrow.","Thrombocytopenia is a platelet deficiency.","Hemophilia is a clotting factor deficiency.","Von Willebrand disease causes poor platelet adhesion.","Thrombosis is pathological clot formation.","Disseminated intravascular coagulation involves systemic activation of coagulation with microvascular thrombosis and consumption of platelets and clotting factors, so bleeding and organ dysfunction may also occur."],"s":[0,3.28,10.32,14.54,18.76,21.11,23.92,27.21,29.55],"f":[0,3.16,9.94,14.01,18.08,20.33,23.05,26.21,28.47]},{"t":["These are the tests of the blood and lymphatic system, and many of them are done in the laboratory where I work.","The C B C, or complete blood count, counts all the blood cells.","The peripheral blood smear lets us look at the cells under the microscope.","The coagulation profile includes the P T, the a P T T and the I N R.","Blood typing and cross-match check A B O and R h compatibility.","Bone marrow aspiration takes a sample of marrow.","Flow cytometry identifies cell types.","A lymph node biopsy takes lymph node tissue.","And serologic and molecular tests look for antibodies and genes."],"s":[0,6.56,10.8,14.86,20.45,25.01,28.45,31.45,34.76],"f":[0,5.83,9.77,13.63,18.66,22.85,25.91,28.63,31.5]},{"t":["Finally, the procedures and drug classes.","Phlebotomy means drawing blood, and venipuncture is the puncture of a vein to do it.","A bone marrow biopsy takes a core sample of marrow.","Transfusion is giving blood components.","For drugs: iron preparations treat iron deficiency.","E S As, erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, increase red cell production.","Anticoagulants prevent clotting.","And antiplatelet agents block platelet aggregation.","That completes the content of this lecture."],"s":[0,2.92,8.15,11.83,14.79,18.39,23.82,26.22,29.81],"f":[0,2.82,7.58,10.85,13.6,16.89,21.99,24.19,27.54]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 1 to 5.","Question 1. Pain along a nerve: A, Neuropathy. B, Neuritis. C, Neuralgia. D, Analgesia. The answer is C, Neuralgia.","Question 2. Inflammation of the brain: A, Meningitis. B, Neuritis. C, Myelitis. D, Encephalitis. The answer is D, Encephalitis.","Question 3. Inflammation of the meninges: A, Aphasia. B, Encephalitis. C, Neuropathy. D, Meningitis. The answer is D, Meningitis.","Question 4. Abnormal fear: A, Phobia. B, Mania. C, Dementia. D, Psychosis. The answer is A, Phobia.","Question 5. Loss of memory: A, Ataxia. B, Aphasia. C, Amnesia. D, Insomnia. The answer is C, Amnesia."],"s":[0,2.6,16.77,32.09,47.61,60.93],"f":[0,2.53,15.51,29.74,44,56.26]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. Impaired language or speech: A, Vertigo. B, Aphasia. C, Tremor. D, Syncope. The answer is B, Aphasia.","Question 7. Brief loss of consciousness: A, Seizure. B, Paralysis. C, Syncope. D, Migraine. The answer is C, Syncope.","Question 8. Lack of pain sensation: A, Hyperesthesia. B, Neuralgia. C, Analgesia. D, Paresthesia. The answer is C, Analgesia.","Question 9. Persistent low mood: A, Depression. B, Mania. C, Anxiety. D, Delusion. The answer is A, Depression.","Question 10. False fixed belief: A, Hallucination. B, Compulsion. C, Delusion. D, Obsession. The answer is C, Delusion."],"s":[0,2.61,16.63,30.68,45.62,59.39],"f":[0,2.45,15.31,28.3,41.99,54.52]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 11 to 15.","Question 11. Which two structures form the CNS? A, Cranial and spinal nerves. B, Brain and spinal cord. C, Muscles and glands. D, Sympathetic, parasympathetic. The answer is B, Brain and spinal cord.","Question 12. Which part of the neuron receives signals? A, Axon. B, Synaptic vesicle. C, Myelin sheath. D, Dendrite. The answer is D, Dendrite.","Question 13. Which part carries impulses away from the cell body? A, Dendrite. B, Axon. C, Nucleus. D, Neuroglia. The answer is B, Axon.","Question 14. Which cells form myelin in the CNS? A, Oligodendrocytes. B, Schwann cells. C, Astrocytes. D, Microglia. The answer is A, Oligodendrocytes.","Question 15. Which cells form myelin in the PNS? A, Oligodendrocytes. B, Schwann cells. C, Ependymal cells. D, Astrocytes. The answer is B, Schwann cells."],"s":[0,3.14,22.4,38.47,54.9,72.33],"f":[0,2.68,20.46,35.47,50.62,66.49]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 16 to 20.","Question 16. Lobe for voluntary motor control: A, Frontal. B, Parietal. C, Temporal. D, Occipital. The answer is A, Frontal.","Question 17. Lobe for vision: A, Frontal. B, Parietal. C, Temporal. D, Occipital. The answer is D, Occipital.","Question 18. Coordinates balance and movement: A, Cerebellum. B, Thalamus. C, Hypothalamus. D, Medulla. The answer is A, Cerebellum.","Question 19. Fight or flight division: A, Somatic. B, Parasympathetic. C, Sympathetic. D, Enteric. The answer is C, Sympathetic.","Question 20. Relays sensory information to the cortex: A, Cerebellum. B, Thalamus. C, Pons. D, Spinal cord. The answer is B, Thalamus."],"s":[0,2.99,17.44,31.09,45.83,60.72],"f":[0,2.71,16.01,28.75,42.41,55.89]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 21 to 25.","Question 21. Deficiency of red blood cells: A, Leukopenia. B, Thrombocytopenia. C, Polycythemia. D, Erythropenia. The answer is D, Erythropenia.","Question 22. Abnormally low platelets: A, Thrombocytosis. B, Thrombocytopenia. C, Leukocytosis. D, Erythrocytosis. The answer is B, Thrombocytopenia.","Question 23. Main oxygen-carrying cell: A, Platelet. B, Leukocyte. C, Erythrocyte. D, Plasma cell. The answer is C, Erythrocyte.","Question 24. Enlargement of the spleen: A, Splenomegaly. B, Lymphadenopathy. C, Hepatomegaly. D, Adenopathy. The answer is A, Splenomegaly.","Question 25. Excessive bleeding: A, Thrombosis. B, Hemorrhage. C, Hemolysis. D, Coagulation. The answer is B, Hemorrhage."],"s":[0,3.23,19.93,36.88,51.96,67.78],"f":[0,3.05,18.4,33.9,47.93,62.48]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 26 to 30.","Question 26. Cell for antibody production: A, Neutrophil. B, Eosinophil. C, Plasma cell. D, Monocyte. The answer is C, Plasma cell.","Question 27. Reduction in white blood cells: A, Leukocytosis. B, Lymphocytosis. C, Neutrophilia. D, Leukopenia. The answer is D, Leukopenia.","Question 28. Malignancy of white blood cells: A, Anemia. B, Leukemia. C, Lymphadenitis. D, Thrombocytopenia. The answer is B, Leukemia.","Question 29. Inflammation of a lymph node: A, Splenitis. B, Lymphadenitis. C, Hepatitis. D, Phlebitis. The answer is B, Lymphadenitis.","Question 30. Disorder of clotting factors: A, Thrombophilia. B, Hemostasis. C, Polycythemia. D, Coagulopathy. The answer is D, Coagulopathy."],"s":[0,2.98,18.13,34.28,49.97,65.64],"f":[0,2.73,16.8,31.78,46.35,60.86]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 31 to 35.","Question 31. Enlarged lymph nodes: A, Lymphadenopathy. B, Lymphopenia. C, Thrombosis. D, Neutropenia. The answer is A, Lymphadenopathy.","Question 32. Main component for clot formation: A, Plasma cell. B, Erythrocyte. C, Platelet. D, Lymphocyte. The answer is C, Platelet.","Question 33. Deficiency of hemoglobin or red cells: A, Leukocytosis. B, Polycythemia. C, Anemia. D, Hemolysis. The answer is C, Anemia.","Question 34. Organ that removes old red cells: A, Thymus. B, Pancreas. C, Thyroid. D, Spleen. The answer is D, Spleen.","Question 35. Increased white cell count: A, Leukocytosis. B, Leukopenia. C, Thrombocytopenia. D, Anemia. The answer is A, Leukocytosis."],"s":[0,3.25,19.27,34.26,50.43,65.21],"f":[0,3.04,17.6,31.49,46.36,59.98]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 36 to 40.","Question 36. Malignant plasma cells: A, Myeloma. B, Lymphoma. C, Hemophilia. D, Splenitis. The answer is A, Myeloma.","Question 37. Becomes a macrophage in tissues: A, Erythrocyte. B, Eosinophil. C, Basophil. D, Monocyte. The answer is D, Monocyte.","Question 38. Tendency to form abnormal clots: A, Hemorrhage. B, Thrombophilia. C, Coagulopathy. D, Lymphadenitis. The answer is B, Thrombophilia.","Question 39. Caused by clotting-factor deficiency: A, Thrombosis. B, Leukemia. C, Polycythemia. D, Hemophilia. The answer is D, Hemophilia.","Question 40. Primary site of blood cell production: A, Bone marrow. B, Spleen. C, Lymph node. D, Liver. The answer is A, Bone marrow."],"s":[0,3.01,17.78,33.01,49.22,64.87],"f":[0,2.72,16.4,30.56,45.66,60.02]}],"8":[{"t":["Welcome to lecture eight, our final lecture.","Today we study the skin, the largest organ of the body, and the special senses: sight, hearing, balance, smell and taste.","These are the systems that connect us to the world around us.","As always, we will learn the roots first, then the structures, the signs, the disorders, and the tests and treatments."],"s":[0,2.92,10.44,14.22],"f":[0,2.72,9.82,13.24]},{"t":["By the end of this lecture you should be able to do four things.","First, use the skin and special sense word roots, such as dermo, ophthalmo, oto and gusto.","Second, describe the main structures of the skin, eye and ear, while recognizing basic terminology of smell and taste.","Third, recognise skin lesions and sensory disorders.","And fourth, know the main tests, procedures and drug classes.","At the end, you will test yourself with forty multiple-choice questions."],"s":[0,5.53,11.86,19.37,22.13,26.08],"f":[0,5.48,11.74,19.17,21.91,25.82]},{"t":["The diagram shows three regions: epidermis, dermis and the hypodermis. Strictly speaking, the skin proper consists of the epidermis and dermis; the hypodermis is subcutaneous tissue beneath it.","The epidermis is the outer protective layer.","Below it is the dermis, which contains the blood vessels, nerves, glands and hair follicles.","And the deepest layer is the hypodermis, the fat layer under the skin, which gives insulation and cushioning.","Notice the prefixes: epi means upon, and hypo means under, so the epidermis is upon the dermis, and the hypodermis is under it."],"s":[0,11.37,14.21,20.3,27.6],"f":[0,11.01,13.76,19.65,26.73]},{"t":["Here are the skin roots.","Derm-o, dermat-o and cutane-o all refer to skin, as in dermatology and cutaneous.","Hypodermo means under the skin, as in a hypodermic needle.","Tricho and pilo mean hair, as in trichology.","Onycho means nail, as in onychomycosis, a fungal infection of the nail.","Sebo is sebum, the skin oil, as in sebaceous.","Hidro means sweat, as in hidradenitis.","Be careful not to confuse hidro, sweat, with hydro, water.","Xero means dry, as in xerosis.","Lipo means fat, as in lipoma.","Kerato means keratin, as in keratosis.","Melano means dark pigment, as in melanoma.","And angio means vessel, as in angioma."],"s":[0,2.31,8.32,12.94,16.63,22.18,26.34,29.11,33.73,36.5,39.27,42.05,45.28],"f":[0,2.13,7.66,11.92,15.32,20.43,24.26,26.81,31.06,33.62,36.17,38.72,41.7]},{"t":["Melanocytes are special cells in the basal layer of the epidermis, and they make melanin, the pigment that gives skin its colour.","Follow the three steps.","First, melanin is produced inside small packages called melanosomes.","Second, the melanosomes are transferred through the cell's long arms to the surrounding skin cells, the keratinocytes.","Third, the melanin spreads in the keratinocytes and gives colour.","Darker skin has more melanin, in greater concentration, and lighter skin has less.","Melanin also protects the skin by absorbing ultraviolet light from the sun."],"s":[0,7.74,9.44,14.58,21.81,26.01,31.36],"f":[0,7.08,8.71,13.36,19.88,23.56,28.3]},{"t":["The hair grows from a hair follicle in the dermis.","The hair root is below the skin, and the hair shaft is above it.","At the base is the hair bulb, nourished by the dermal papilla.","Attached to the follicle are the sebaceous gland, which oils the hair, and the arrector pili muscle, which makes the hair stand up and gives us goosebumps.","The hair shaft has three layers: the cuticle outside, the cortex, which gives strength and colour, and the medulla, the inner core.","Hair grows in a cycle: anagen, the growth phase; catagen, the transition phase; telogen, the resting phase; and exogen, the shedding phase."],"s":[0,3.21,6.95,10.84,19.9,27.59],"f":[0,2.9,6.23,9.58,17.73,24.23]},{"t":["The nail has five main parts.","The nail plate is the hard part we see.","The nail bed lies under it.","The cuticle is the skin fold at the base.","The nail matrix is where the nail grows from, and the lunula is the white half-moon at the base of the nail.","The skin also has two types of gland.","Sebaceous glands make oil, called sebum, and open into the hair follicles.","Sweat glands cool the body, and open directly onto the skin surface."],"s":[0,2.23,4.98,7.03,9.92,16.34,19.12,24],"f":[0,2.06,4.56,6.44,9.09,14.8,17.31,21.77]},{"t":["Skin lesions are changes in the skin caused by disease or injury, and we describe them with exact words.","A macule is a flat, nonpalpable area of altered color usually less than one centimetre across, such as a freckle. A larger flat lesion is commonly called a patch.","A papule is a small solid bump, less than one centimetre, and a plaque is a raised, flat-topped lesion, more than one centimetre.","A nodule is a solid, palpable lesion, often larger than one centimetre and extending into the dermis or subcutis.","A pustule is filled with pus.","A vesicle is a small blister, less than one centimetre, and a bulla is a large blister, more than one centimetre.","A wheal is an itchy raised area, a hive.","An erosion is loss of the epidermis, while an ulcer is a deeper loss into the dermis.","A crust is dried serum or blood.","And a scar is fibrous tissue that replaces normal skin."],"s":[0,6.74,17.03,25.19,31.93,34.06,41.51,44.7,50.74,53.22],"f":[0,6.41,16.2,23.96,30.38,32.4,39.49,42.53,48.27,50.63]},{"t":["These are the common signs seen on the skin.","Erythema is redness: erythro means red.","Edema is swelling.","Pruritus is itching.","Dryness means dry, scaly skin, which we call xerosis.","Warmth means increased skin temperature.","Tenderness is pain when the skin is touched.","And discoloration is a change in skin colour.","Notice that redness, swelling, warmth and pain together are the classic signs of inflammation."],"s":[0,2.71,5.71,7.27,8.94,12.95,15.72,18.41,21.66],"f":[0,2.52,5.28,6.7,8.26,12.05,14.51,16.95,19.74]},{"t":["How do we examine and treat skin lesions?","A biopsy removes a small piece of skin to look at under the microscope.","Dermoscopy magnifies the skin structures.","Patch testing helps identify delayed-type contact allergens in suspected allergic contact dermatitis.","A Wood's lamp uses ultraviolet light to support the assessment of selected infections and pigmentary changes, but it is not a definitive test on its own.","A culture identifies infectious agents.","For treatment: incision and drainage opens and drains a collection of pus.","Excision removes a lesion completely.","Cryotherapy freezes abnormal tissue: cryo means cold.","Curettage scrapes away tissue.","And suturing closes a wound with stitches."],"s":[0,3.32,9.13,11.2,16.18,26.96,29.04,34.01,36.09,38.99,40.65],"f":[0,3.16,8.7,10.68,15.43,25.71,27.69,32.44,34.41,37.18,38.77]},{"t":["These are the main drug groups for skin disorders.","Topical corticosteroids reduce inflammation and itching.","Antibiotics treat bacterial infections, antifungals treat fungal infections, and antivirals treat viral skin infections.","Antihistamines relieve itching and allergy.","And retinoids normalize the growth of skin cells, and are used for acne and psoriasis."],"s":[0,3.28,7.27,15.75,18.7],"f":[0,2.89,6.59,14.17,17.04]},{"t":["Now we move to the special senses, five specialized systems that connect us to the world.","Vision detects light and forms images.","Hearing detects sound vibrations.","Equilibrium senses the position of the head and keeps our balance.","Smell detects odours in the air.","And taste detects flavours.","Notice that the ear has two jobs: hearing and balance."],"s":[0,5.88,8.93,11.39,15.5,17.77,19.79],"f":[0,5.26,7.8,10.03,13.71,15.78,17.69]},{"t":["Here are the eye roots.","Ophthalmo and oculo both mean eye, as in ophthalmology and ocular.","Opto means vision, as in optometry.","Retino is the retina, as in retinopathy.","Corneo is the cornea, as in corneal ulcer.","Irido is the iris, as in iridectomy.","Pupillo is the pupil.","Blepharo means eyelid, as in blepharitis.","Lacrim-o refers to tears or the lacrimal apparatus, as in the lacrimal gland and lacrimal duct.","And photo means light, as in photophobia, sensitivity to light."],"s":[0,2.19,6.99,9.62,12.67,16.17,19.23,20.98,23.6,30.59],"f":[0,2.08,6.67,9.17,12.09,15.42,18.34,20.01,22.51,29.18]},{"t":["Now the ear roots.","Oto and auro both mean ear, as in otitis and auricle.","Audio means hearing, as in audiology, and acouso means sound, as in acoustics.","Tympano and myringo both mean the eardrum, as in tympanometry and myringitis.","Ossiculo refers to the ossicles, the small bones of the middle ear, as in ossiculoplasty.","Cochleo is the cochlea, as in cochlear implant.","Vestibul-o refers to the vestibule of the inner ear, involved in balance, as in vestibulocochlear nerve.","And mastoido is the mastoid bone behind the ear, as in mastoiditis."],"s":[0,1.74,6.54,12.2,17.43,23.97,27.45,34.43],"f":[0,1.61,6.03,11.26,16.08,22.11,25.33,31.76]},{"t":["These roots describe smell, taste and the mouth.","Olfact-o and osm-o refer to smell, as in olfaction and anosmia, the loss of smell. Osm-o should not be confused with the unrelated osmosis.","Rhino and naso mean nose, as in rhinitis and nasal.","Gusto and geusio both mean taste, as in gustatory.","Glosso and linguo both mean tongue, as in glossitis and lingual.","Sialo means saliva, as in sialadenitis, inflammation of a salivary gland.","And stomato means the mouth, as in stomatitis."],"s":[0,3.4,13.61,17.87,21.69,26.37,31.05],"f":[0,3.21,12.85,16.87,20.49,24.91,29.32]},{"t":["This slide shows the eyeball.","Light enters through the cornea, the clear protective window at the front.","The iris is the coloured part that controls how much light enters, and the pupil is the opening in its centre.","The lens, behind the pupil, focuses the light, and the ciliary body changes the shape of the lens and produces the aqueous humor.","The light then crosses the vitreous body, which maintains the shape of the eye, and reaches the retina, which detects light.","The macula and fovea give sharp central vision.","The optic nerve carries the visual signals to the brain.","The outer coat is the tough white sclera, and the choroid, under the retina, nourishes the eye."],"s":[0,2.09,6.49,13.27,21.59,28.85,32.07,35.76],"f":[0,1.8,5.8,11.97,19.37,25.97,28.82,32.1]},{"t":["The ear has three parts.","The external ear, the auricle and the ear canal, collects sound.","The middle ear transmits vibration: the sound makes the eardrum vibrate, and three tiny bones, the ossicles, pass the vibration inward.","The inner ear converts the vibration into nerve signals.","The snail-shaped cochlea is for hearing, and the semicircular canals and vestibule are for balance."],"s":[0,1.94,5.89,14.54,18.28],"f":[0,1.72,5.31,13.26,16.58]},{"t":["Taste and smell are chemical senses.","Taste is detected by taste buds on the tongue.","Each taste bud contains receptor cells that send signals through nerves to the brain.","There are five basic tastes, shown at the bottom of the slide: sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami, the savoury taste.","Smell is detected by olfactory receptor cells in the olfactory epithelium, high in the roof of the nose.","They send signals to the olfactory bulb, the first relay centre, and then along the olfactory tract to the brain.","Much of what we call flavour actually comes from smell."],"s":[0,2.65,5.82,11.17,18.75,25.82,32.79],"f":[0,2.38,5.41,10.42,17.47,24.01,30.29]},{"t":["These are common problems of the eye and sight.","In myopia, or short-sightedness, distant vision is blurred.","In hyperopia, or long-sightedness, near vision is blurred.","Astigmatism is an irregular focus, usually from an unevenly curved cornea.","A cataract is a cloudy lens.","Glaucoma is a group of progressive optic neuropathies, often associated with increased intraocular pressure, but it can occur with pressure in the normal range.","And conjunctivitis is a red, inflamed conjunctiva, often called pink eye."],"s":[0,4,7.55,11.1,15.98,18.65,29.3],"f":[0,3.9,7.36,10.83,15.6,18.19,28.59]},{"t":["These are common problems of the ear.","Otitis media is a middle ear infection, very common in children.","Hearing loss means reduced hearing.","Tinnitus is ringing in the ear.","Vertigo is a spinning sensation.","Ménière disease is vertigo with hearing symptoms, such as tinnitus and hearing loss.","And otosclerosis is fixation of the stapes, the last ossicle, which causes hearing loss."],"s":[0,2.53,7.14,9.5,11.68,14.21,19.6],"f":[0,2.22,6.39,8.55,10.48,12.64,17.67]},{"t":["Smell and taste disorders can be a loss, a reduction or a distortion.","For smell, with the root osm: anosmia is loss of smell, hyposmia is reduced smell, and parosmia is distorted smell.","For taste, using the combining form geus-i or the suffix geusia: ageusia is loss of taste, hypogeusia is reduced taste, and dysgeusia is distorted taste.","Notice the same pattern for both senses: a or an for none, hypo for less, and para or dys for abnormal."],"s":[0,5.49,13.93,24.49],"f":[0,5.22,13.26,23.3]},{"t":["How do we test vision, hearing and the structures of the head?","A visual acuity test measures how clearly a person sees.","Ophthalmoscopy views the retina at the back of the eye.","Tonometry measures the pressure inside the eye, which is important for glaucoma.","Audiometry measures the hearing threshold.","Tympanometry tests the mobility of the eardrum.","And C T or M R I gives structural images."],"s":[0,3.47,7.46,11.06,16.05,18.92,22.19],"f":[0,3.17,6.81,10.03,14.62,17.32,20.21]},{"t":["These are the eye, ear and nose procedures.","Cataract extraction removes the cloudy lens and inserts an artificial lens.","L A S I K stands for laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis. It uses a laser to reshape the cornea and correct selected refractive errors.","Tympanoplasty is the repair of the eardrum: plasty means repair.","Myringotomy is an incision into the eardrum: otomy means incision.","A cochlear implant is a device that stimulates the auditory nerve.","And nasal endoscopy uses a scope to view the nose and sinuses."],"s":[0,3.27,7.76,17.56,21.65,25.73,30.23],"f":[0,3.24,7.69,17.4,21.44,25.49,29.94]},{"t":["Finally, the eye, ear and nose drugs.","Mydriatics dilate the pupil, and miotics constrict it.","Ophthalmic antibiotics treat bacterial eye infections when indicated, not viral or noninfectious eye disorders; topical ophthalmic anesthetics numb the eye surface for procedures.","Otic antibiotics treat ear infections.","Cerumenolytics soften earwax: cerumen is earwax, and lytic means breaking down.","Otic steroids reduce ear inflammation.","Antihistamines relieve allergy.","Intranasal steroids reduce nasal inflammation, and decongestants relieve nasal congestion.","That completes the content of our final lecture."],"s":[0,3.53,7.57,19.18,21.7,27.26,29.78,31.29,36.34],"f":[0,3.41,7.31,18.52,20.96,26.32,28.75,30.21,35.09]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 1 to 5.","Question 1. Combining form meaning skin: A, trich/o. B, hidr/o. C, onych/o. D, dermat/o. The answer is D, dermat/o.","Question 2. Inflammation of the skin: A, Dermatitis. B, Dermatosis. C, Dermatology. D, Dermatoplasty. The answer is A, Dermatitis.","Question 3. Combining form for hair: A, seb/o. B, kerat/o. C, trich/o. D, melan/o. The answer is C, trich/o.","Question 4. Fungal infection of the nail: A, Onycholysis. B, Onychomycosis. C, Onychitis. D, Onychophagia. The answer is B, Onychomycosis.","Question 5. Combining form meaning sweat: A, hidr/o. B, xer/o. C, lip/o. D, erythr/o. The answer is A, hidr/o."],"s":[0,2.6,16.44,32.22,45.76,61.68],"f":[0,2.53,15.37,29.61,42.16,56.75]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 6 to 10.","Question 6. Abnormal dryness of the skin: A, Erythema. B, Pruritus. C, Xerosis. D, Edema. The answer is C, Xerosis.","Question 7. Redness of the skin: A, Cyanosis. B, Pallor. C, Ecchymosis. D, Erythema. The answer is D, Erythema.","Question 8. Term for itching: A, Pruritus. B, Alopecia. C, Urticaria. D, Petechia. The answer is A, Pruritus.","Question 9. Combining form for sebum (oil): A, hidr/o. B, seb/o. C, kerat/o. D, cutane/o. The answer is B, seb/o.","Question 10. Excessive keratin formation: A, Keratosis. B, Melanosis. C, Dermatitis. D, Lipoma. The answer is A, Keratosis."],"s":[0,2.6,16.85,30.7,44.46,58.48],"f":[0,2.45,15.83,28.78,41.39,54.4]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 11 to 15.","Question 11. Outermost layer of the skin: A, Dermis. B, Hypodermis. C, Epidermis. D, Fascia. The answer is C, Epidermis.","Question 12. Epidermal cell that makes melanin: A, Keratinocyte. B, Melanocyte. C, Fibroblast. D, Adipocyte. The answer is B, Melanocyte.","Question 13. Layer with vessels, glands, receptors: A, Dermis. B, Epidermis. C, Stratum corneum. D, Nail plate. The answer is A, Dermis.","Question 14. Gland that produces sebum: A, Eccrine gland. B, Apocrine gland. C, Sebaceous gland. D, Endocrine gland. The answer is C, Sebaceous gland.","Question 15. Receptor for deep pressure, vibration: A, Merkel disc. B, Meissner corpuscle. C, Free nerve ending. D, Pacinian corpuscle. The answer is D, Pacinian corpuscle."],"s":[0,3.14,17.77,32.98,48.36,64.85],"f":[0,2.68,16.12,30.47,44.68,59.78]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 16 to 20.","Question 16. Response that increases heat loss: A, Vasoconstriction. B, Vasodilation. C, Piloerection. D, Reduced sweating. The answer is B, Vasodilation.","Question 17. Small solid raised lesion smaller than 1 cm: A, Macule. B, Ulcer. C, Bulla. D, Papule. The answer is D, Papule.","Question 18. Small fluid-filled lesion: A, Nodule. B, Plaque. C, Vesicle. D, Wheal. The answer is C, Vesicle.","Question 19. Structure that makes new nail cells: A, Nail plate. B, Nail bed. C, Free edge. D, Nail matrix. The answer is D, Nail matrix.","Question 20. Magnified exam of pigmented lesions: A, Patch testing. B, Dermoscopy. C, Cryotherapy. D, Curettage. The answer is B, Dermoscopy."],"s":[0,2.99,19.18,34.28,47.87,63.23],"f":[0,2.71,17.86,32.04,44.76,59.06]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 21 to 25.","Question 21. Converts sound vibrations into signals: A, Cochlea. B, Semicircular canals. C, Vestibule. D, Otolith organs. The answer is A, Cochlea.","Question 22. Detects rotational head movement; helps balance: A, Cochlea. B, Cochlear nerve. C, Vestibule. D, Semicircular canals. The answer is D, Semicircular canals.","Question 23. Senses linear acceleration and gravity: A, Semicircular canals. B, Cochlea. C, Otolith organs. D, Cochlear nerve. The answer is C, Otolith organs.","Question 24. Nerve carrying hearing to the brain: A, Vestibular nerve. B, Cochlear nerve. C, Trigeminal nerve. D, Facial nerve. The answer is B, Cochlear nerve.","Question 25. Sense associated with the cochlea: A, Hearing. B, Balance. C, Smell. D, Taste. The answer is A, Hearing."],"s":[0,3.23,19.7,36.96,53.98,70.42],"f":[0,3.05,17.99,33.88,49.46,64.64]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 26 to 30.","Question 26. Sense linked to the vestibular apparatus: A, Hearing. B, Balance. C, Smell. D, Taste. The answer is B, Balance.","Question 27. Three looped, fluid-filled canals of the inner ear: A, Otolith organs. B, Tympanic membrane. C, Cochlea. D, Semicircular canals. The answer is D, Semicircular canals.","Question 28. Hair cells that respond to sound: A, Cochlea. B, Otolith organs. C, Vestibule. D, Semicircular canals. The answer is A, Cochlea.","Question 29. Damage causing rotational vertigo: A, Cochlea. B, Semicircular canals. C, Otolith organs. D, Eustachian tube. The answer is B, Semicircular canals.","Question 30. Correct structure–function pair: A, Cochlea — balance. B, Semicircular canals — hearing. C, Otoliths — linear acceleration. D, Cochlear nerve — rotation. The answer is C, Otoliths — linear acceleration."],"s":[0,2.98,17.1,35.02,50.98,68.2],"f":[0,2.73,16.11,32.66,47.12,62.99]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 31 to 35.","Question 31. Primary receptor for taste: A, Taste buds. B, Papillae. C, Olfactory epithelium. D, Trigeminal ganglion. The answer is A, Taste buds.","Question 32. Nerve for taste, anterior two-thirds of tongue: A, cranial nerve 7. B, cranial nerve 9. C, cranial nerve 10. D, cranial nerve 5. The answer is A, cranial nerve 7.","Question 33. Taste triggered by glutamate: A, Bitter. B, Sour. C, Sweet. D, Umami. The answer is D, Umami.","Question 34. Receptor cell for dissolved chemicals: A, Rod cells. B, Photoreceptors. C, Gustatory cells. D, Mechanoreceptors. The answer is C, Gustatory cells.","Question 35. General sensation to anterior two-thirds of tongue: A, cranial nerve 7. B, cranial nerve 9. C, cranial nerve 10. D, cranial nerve 5. The answer is D, cranial nerve 5."],"s":[0,3.25,19.48,37.64,51.53,68.56],"f":[0,3.04,17.94,34.63,47.4,62.94]},{"t":["Check yourself, questions 36 to 40.","Question 36. Ear part containing the hearing organ: A, External canal. B, Tympanic membrane. C, Cochlea. D, Eustachian tube. The answer is C, Cochlea.","Question 37. Mainly responsible for balance: A, Cochlea. B, Semicircular canals. C, Organ of Corti. D, Ossicles. The answer is B, Semicircular canals.","Question 38. NOT a basic taste quality: A, Sweet. B, Salty. C, Umami. D, Metallic. The answer is D, Metallic.","Question 39. Loss of smell after a cold: A, Parosmia. B, Hyposmia. C, Anosmia. D, Ageusia. The answer is C, Anosmia.","Question 40. Unpleasant distorted taste: A, Ageusia. B, Dysgeusia. C, Hypogeusia. D, Otorrhea. The answer is B, Dysgeusia."],"s":[0,3.01,19.05,35.77,50.07,65.06],"f":[0,2.72,17.67,33.06,46.28,59.96]}]};